/// The kernel module: `Object` and the classes every program uses without
/// importing them -- the numbers, Strings and Symbols, `Array`, `Closure`,
/// the Booleans, nil's class, the classes of class objects and the
/// exceptions. The host instantiates it once, at boot.
///
/// The core library (`docs/design/core-library.md`, "The core library as
/// Newspeak source over a VM mirror"). One nested class per builtin class;
/// the bootstrap fills the builtin class objects from them before it
/// instantiates this module, so a nested class here *is* the class a literal
/// has. Beside them, ordinary classes, which their accessors build as any
/// class's do. Every method that needs a leaf reaches Rust through
/// `vmMirror`, the one object that holds the leaves, and nothing else here
/// does.
///
/// A builtin's superclass clause names a sibling by its bare name and
/// nothing else, and so does each of its `<:` clauses, which may name only
/// an ordinary class whose own clause is a bare builtin name: the bootstrap
/// reads the names and runs neither clause, because the classes they name
/// must exist before any clause could run. An ordinary class's clause runs
/// when the boot builds the class. `Top` declares none: it has no
/// superclass.
///
/// Every class here says `public`: §6.3.1 makes an undeclared member
/// protected, and a protected class is unreachable from another module, so
/// the accessibility has to be written out for a program to name it. `Top`
/// is the exception, for the reason its own comment gives.
///
/// The kernel declares itself a value so that every builtin value class,
/// nested here, meets §3.1.1's enclosing-object condition.
///
/// @factory makes the kernel module over `mirror`, which it copies into
/// `vmMirror` and hands to no other object; the host sends it once, at boot, after
/// the bootstrap has filled the builtin class objects from the nested
/// classes.
/// @param mirror the VM mirror, which the host alone holds; every native
/// operation of these classes is a send to it.
(* :module: newspeak.core.Kernel *)
class Kernel vmMirror: mirror = Value (
  |
    /// The VM mirror the factory was given: the one object through which
    /// a method here reaches a native operation. Never handed out.
    private vmMirror = mirror.
  |
) (
  /// The empty class, `Object`'s superclass: it declares nothing, and no
  /// program can name it.
  ///
  /// §3.2: "A class is either the empty class `Top` or the application of
  /// a mixin to another class known as its superclass", and "Only the
  /// class `Object` may have `Top` as its superclass" -- which is why it
  /// is private, as are only the classes here that no program may name:
  /// no other module may name it. It stays empty, so that all code,
  /// `Object`'s included, resides in a mixin.
  private class Top = () ()

  /// The root of every class a program declares: the protocol every object
  /// answers -- its identity, equality, hash and printed form, the nil
  /// protocol, `halt` and `doesNotUnderstand:` -- and, to itself alone, its
  /// class and the class accessors.
  public class Object = Top () (
    /// Reads the receiver's class, to the receiver's own sends only.
    ///
    /// Protected, so that holding an instance does not hand out its
    /// class -- its factory, its class side's state, a subclass in the
    /// instance's environment: `self class` and `super class` reach it,
    /// `other class` falls to `doesNotUnderstand:`. On the class side
    /// `self class` answers the metaclass.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's class: the class object it was
    /// instantiated from, a value class's per enclosing object.
    protected class = (
      ^vmMirror objectClass: self ifFail: [^super class].
    )

    /// Tests whether `other` is the receiver itself: identity, the same
    /// object. It sends no message to either side and never signals
    /// itself; only forcing a future, below, runs any code.
    ///
    /// Every object is identical to itself and to nothing else, however
    /// equal another object is to it: `Object new == Object new` is
    /// false. Identity is an equivalence relation -- reflexive, symmetric,
    /// transitive -- and holds for as long as the objects live: a
    /// collection moves objects, an install of a MirrorBuilder migrates
    /// them to a new layout and an image carries them to another process,
    /// and none of them makes two objects identical or one object two.
    /// `identityHash` agrees with it. A future on either side stands for
    /// its value (§6.3.2): it is forced -- its computation run the first
    /// time -- and the value is compared in its place.
    ///
    /// `==` is a message, so a class may declare its own, and this method
    /// answers only for an object that is no value. A value's class
    /// inherits `Value>>==`, which sends `=`, so for a value identity is
    /// equality: an immediate -- a SmallInteger, a SmallFloat64, `true`,
    /// `false`, `nil` -- is identical to every value equal to it, and so
    /// is a boxed Float, a LargeInteger, a Fraction, a String or a Symbol:
    /// `3 == 3.0`, `0.0 == -0.0` and two separately computed equal
    /// LargeIntegers are true. §3.1.1 makes a class below `Value` that
    /// declares `==` no value class, and the runtime refuses to instantiate
    /// one ('cannot instantiate <class>: a value class may not define ==,
    /// and <declaring class> defines it', naming the class instantiated
    /// and the class of the chain that declares `==`). A class that is no value may declare `==`
    /// -- `Behavior` does, so that a class object of a value answers what
    /// its `=` does -- and its `==` then answers what its declaration says.
    /// A `Proxy` answers `==` from outside through its `doesNotUnderstand:`
    /// (`Proxy>>==`), and a proxy that forwards it to its target breaks
    /// the equivalence: it is not identical to itself from outside, and
    /// its target is not identical to it.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is the receiver itself, a Boolean; for a
    /// future, whether its value is.
    /// @see `=`, `identityHash` and `Value>>==`.
    public == other = (
      ^vmMirror object: self identicalTo: other ifFail: [^super == other].
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` for equality: here by identity,
    /// so an object whose class declares no `=` of its own equals only
    /// itself. It sends no message to either side and never signals
    /// itself; a future on either side stands for its value, as for `==`.
    ///
    /// The contract: a class that declares `=` makes it an equivalence
    /// relation over every object, and the core library -- `Map`, `Set`,
    /// `includes:`, `indexOf:` -- relies on it:
    ///
    /// - reflexive: `x = x` is true;
    /// - symmetric: `x = y` is true exactly when `y = x` is;
    /// - transitive: `x = y` and `y = z` make `x = z` true;
    /// - consistent: `x = y` answers the same on every send while neither
    ///   side's state that `=` reads changes, and a value's never changes;
    /// - `x = nil` is false for every `x` but `nil` itself and what stands
    ///   for it, a future whose value is nil or a proxy forwarding to nil;
    /// - it answers a Boolean for an argument of any class rather than
    ///   signalling, false for one it does not know;
    /// - it agrees with `hash`: whenever `x = y` is true, `x hash = y hash`
    ///   is too, so a class that declares `=` declares `hash` with it.
    ///
    /// A `Proxy` that forwards `=` to its target breaks the contract from
    /// outside: neither reflexive nor symmetric wherever its target's `=`
    /// is the kernel's (`Proxy>>=`).
    ///
    /// `=` is never true between two objects of unrelated classes unless a
    /// class says so: `Object`'s is identity, `Value`'s compares classes
    /// before slots, a String equals only a String and a Symbol only
    /// itself. The numbers say so: an Integer, a LargeInteger, a Float and
    /// a Fraction are equal by exact value, whatever their classes (`1 =
    /// 1.0`, `(1/2) = 0.5`; `Number>>=`).
    ///
    /// Where `=` and `==` differ: for an object that is no value they
    /// agree until its class declares `=`; then `=` is the class's
    /// equivalence and `==` stays identity, so two equal objects may be
    /// two. For a value (§3.1.1) identity is equality: `Value>>==` sends
    /// `=`, so two equal values are identical. `'abc' == ('abcd' copyFrom:
    /// 1 to: 3)` is true, and so is a String a StringBuilder built against
    /// the literal of its text; two equal LargeIntegers are identical;
    /// `1 == 1.0` is true, as `1 = 1.0` is; a NaN Float is `=` and `==` to
    /// itself and to every other NaN, unlike IEEE 754's comparison; and two
    /// instances of a program's value class whose slots are equal are
    /// identical. An `IdentityMap`, which compares keys with `==`,
    /// therefore holds one key for equal values, as a `Map` does.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is the receiver itself, a Boolean; for a
    /// future, whether its value is.
    /// @see `==`, `hash` and `Value>>=`.
    public = other = (
      ^vmMirror object: self equals: other ifFail: [^super = other].
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver by its identity, so that the hash agrees with
    /// `=`, which is identity here: it answers what `identityHash` does.
    ///
    /// The contract every `hash` keeps: two objects `=` makes equal hash
    /// alike; two unequal ones may hash alike too, which costs a hashed
    /// collection a longer search but never a wrong answer; and an
    /// object's hash stays the same while the state its `=` reads does not
    /// change. It answers an Integer. A class that declares `=` declares
    /// `hash` with it, computed from the state its `=` compares, since a
    /// `Map` and a `Set` find an entry by its `hash` and only then compare
    /// with `=`: a key whose hash disagrees with its `=` is missed by a
    /// lookup with an equal key, and stored a second time beside it. A key
    /// whose hash changes while a Map or a Set holds it may not be found
    /// again.
    /// An `IdentityMap` sends neither: it finds a key by its
    /// `identityHash` and compares with `==`.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's identity hash, an Integer from 1 to
    /// 2^22 - 1 that stays the same for the object's lifetime.
    /// @see `=` and `identityHash`.
    public hash = (
      ^vmMirror objectHash: self ifFail: [^super hash].
    )

    /// Describes the receiver only as an Object: whatever its class, this
    /// method's text is the same.
    ///
    /// @answers the String 'an Object', a new String on every send.
    public printString = (
      ^vmMirror objectPrintString: self ifFail: [^super printString].
    )

    /// Does nothing, for a cascade: `(Array new: 2) at: 1 put: x;
    /// yourself` answers the array, not the last message's answer.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public yourself = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver by its identity, whatever its `hash` is: the
    /// hash that agrees with `==` as `hash` agrees with `=`.
    ///
    /// Identical objects have one identity hash. The reverse does not
    /// hold: the hash is 22 bits wide, so distinct objects may share one.
    /// The counter has 2^22 - 1 numbers, so the 2^22-th object to draw a
    /// hash after another gets that one's again, which two living objects
    /// then share. An object has none
    /// until something asks: the first `identityHash` -- or `hash`, where
    /// it is `Object`'s -- draws the next number of a counter, which skips
    /// 0 and wraps within the 22 bits; nothing about the object's address
    /// or the order objects were made in goes into it. The object keeps it
    /// for its lifetime: across every collection, which moves objects;
    /// across an install of a MirrorBuilder, which migrates an instance to
    /// its new layout and hands it the old one's hash; and through an
    /// image, which carries it verbatim, the counter going on past the
    /// largest hash the image holds when it is loaded.
    ///
    /// A value overrides it with its `hash` (`Value>>identityHash`): a
    /// value's identity is its equality, so its identity hash has to agree
    /// with `=`. An immediate -- a SmallInteger, a SmallFloat64, `true`,
    /// `false`, `nil` -- is a value and answers its `hash`, `3 identityHash`
    /// 3 and `nil identityHash` 0, and two equal Strings answer one
    /// identity hash. An `IdentityMap` finds a key by its `identityHash`
    /// and compares it with `==`, so it treats a value key as a `Map`
    /// does -- equal values are one key -- and every other key by
    /// identity.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's identity hash, an Integer from 1 to
    /// 2^22 - 1, the same on every send for the object's lifetime.
    /// @see `==`, `hash` and `Value>>identityHash`.
    public identityHash = (
      ^vmMirror objectHash: self ifFail: [^super identityHash].
    )

    /// Signals an Error saying that the method which sent it is
    /// abstract. What an abstract method's body sends,
    /// `^self subclassResponsibility`. It never answers: an Error is not
    /// resumable, so a handler can only end the `on:do:` that caught it.
    ///
    /// The name is read off the sending activation, which only the
    /// kernel reaches: a program sees the Error, never the activation.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing; it never returns.
    /// @signals Error 'Collection>>do: is abstract: Nothing must implement
    /// it' -- the class declaring the sending method and its selector,
    /// then the class of the receiver -- or 'a method is abstract: …'
    /// when the sender is no method.
    protected subclassResponsibility = (
      |
        current = vmMirror currentActivation: self ifFail: [nil].
        method = vmMirror activationMethodName: (vmMirror activationSender: current ifFail: [nil])
            ifFail: [nil].
      |
      ^(outer Kernel Error new) signal: (method isNil ifTrue: ['a method'] ifFalse: [method])
          , ' is abstract: '
          , self class name
          , ' must implement it'.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Object` for a bare `Object` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// §3.2: Object "provides ... accessors for predefined classes and
    /// objects, such as the classes of the built-in literals", so a bare
    /// `String` anywhere is the implicit send `self String` and lands
    /// here unless something nearer declares the name. Each body names
    /// the kernel explicitly: inside Object, §5.7's innermost
    /// declaration of `String` is this very method. `Top` and the VM
    /// mirror's class have no accessor.
    ///
    /// Protected, as every accessor here, so that an object hands out no
    /// class through another's accessor: a bare name is an
    /// implicit-receiver send and reaches them from a subclass, a block
    /// and the class side alike; `other Integer` falls to
    /// `doesNotUnderstand:`.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Object`, the same class on every send.
    protected Object = (
      ^outer Kernel Object.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Value` for a bare `Value` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Value`, the same class on every send.
    protected Value = (
      ^outer Kernel Value.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Boolean` for a bare `Boolean` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Boolean`, the same class on every send.
    protected Boolean = (
      ^outer Kernel Boolean.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `True` for a bare `True` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `True`, the same class on every send.
    protected True = (
      ^outer Kernel True.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `False` for a bare `False` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `False`, the same class on every send.
    protected False = (
      ^outer Kernel False.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Number` for a bare `Number` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Number`, the same class on every send.
    protected Number = (
      ^outer Kernel Number.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Integer` for a bare `Integer` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Integer`, the same class on every send.
    protected Integer = (
      ^outer Kernel Integer.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Float` for a bare `Float` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Float`, the same class on every send.
    protected Float = (
      ^outer Kernel Float.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Fraction` for a bare `Fraction` that
    /// nothing nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Fraction`, whose class side makes an
    /// exact quotient of two integers, the same class on every send.
    protected Fraction = (
      ^outer Kernel Fraction.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `String` for a bare `String` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `String`, the same class on every send.
    protected String = (
      ^outer Kernel String.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Symbol` for a bare `Symbol` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Symbol`, the same class on every send.
    protected Symbol = (
      ^outer Kernel Symbol.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `UndefinedObject` for a bare
    /// `UndefinedObject` that nothing nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `UndefinedObject`, nil's class, the
    /// same class on every send.
    protected UndefinedObject = (
      ^outer Kernel UndefinedObject.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Array` for a bare `Array` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Array`, the same class on every send.
    protected Array = (
      ^outer Kernel Array.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `ByteArray` for a bare `ByteArray` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `ByteArray`, the same class on every send.
    protected ByteArray = (
      ^outer Kernel ByteArray.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Closure` for a bare `Closure` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Closure`, the same class on every send.
    protected Closure = (
      ^outer Kernel Closure.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Exception` for a bare `Exception` that
    /// nothing nearer declares.
    ///
    /// `Exception` and `Error` are the two roots a program signals or
    /// catches most; they reach no host, so every object may name them.
    /// The other exception classes stay with `platform kernel`.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Exception`, the root of every
    /// exception: `Exception signal: 'text'` signals a new one. The same
    /// class on every send.
    protected Exception = (
      ^outer Kernel Exception.
    )

    /// Names the kernel's class `Error` for a bare `Error` that nothing
    /// nearer declares.
    ///
    /// @answers the kernel's class `Error`, the exception a handler can
    /// only end, never resume: `Error signal: 'text'` signals a new one.
    /// The same class on every send.
    protected Error = (
      ^outer Kernel Error.
    )

    /// What a send the receiver does not understand runs instead. A
    /// unary selector of eight characters or more that begins with
    /// `isKindOf` answers false. Any other message signals a
    /// `MessageNotUnderstood`; once the exception has reached its default
    /// handler and the run carries on, the message is sent again.
    ///
    /// §5.5: protected, so an explicit-receiver send of it finds nothing
    /// through lookupPublic and falls to the access-blind "defined for"
    /// walk that invokes it for the failing send itself.
    ///
    /// §5.5: Object's default `doesNotUnderstand:` throws a
    /// `MessageNotUnderstood`. If the default handler resumed it -- the
    /// debugger, once the missing method has been created -- the message
    /// is sent again, into the new method. A unary selector that begins
    /// with `isKindOf` answers false instead.
    ///
    /// @param message the `Message` nothing understood.
    /// @answers false for a unary `isKindOf…` selector; for any other, the
    /// value a handler resumes the `MessageNotUnderstood` with, or, once it
    /// has reached its default handler and the run carries on, the answer
    /// of the message sent again.
    /// @signals MessageNotUnderstood carrying `message` and the receiver,
    /// its `messageText` 'an object does not understand #…' naming the
    /// selector, for every message but a unary `isKindOf…` one.
    protected doesNotUnderstand: message = (
      |
        exception
        value
        selector
      |
      /// An `isKindOf…` question nobody declared answers false, so a
      /// program can ask one of any object; a keyword selector is no
      /// such question.
      (message arguments isNil or: [message arguments size = 0]) ifTrue: [
        selector:: message selector asString.
        (selector size >= 8 and: [(selector copyFrom: 1 to: 8) = 'isKindOf']) ifTrue: [^false].
      ].
      exception:: outer Kernel MessageNotUnderstood new.
      exception message: message.
      exception receiver: self.
      exception messageText: 'an object does not understand #' , message selector asString.
      value:: exception signal.
      exception reachedDefaultHandler ifTrue: [^message sendTo: self].
      ^value.
    )

    /// Tells whether the receiver is nil, which it is not.
    ///
    /// Every object but nil is not nil, and `UndefinedObject` answers the
    /// other way. A block for the not-nil case is culled with the
    /// receiver, as the Newspeak corpus relies on, passing `[:x | ...]`
    /// and `[...]` alike. Every body is a quick method
    /// (`docs/design/core-library.md`, "The nil protocol").
    ///
    /// @answers false: the receiver is not nil.
    public isNil = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Tells whether the receiver is anything but nil, which it is.
    ///
    /// @answers true: the receiver is not nil.
    public notNil = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Leaves the receiver as it is: it is not nil, so `block` is not
    /// evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param block any object.
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public ifNil: block = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Evaluates `block`, since the receiver is not nil, giving it the
    /// receiver when it takes an argument.
    ///
    /// @param block a block of no argument or one, or anything
    /// understanding `cull:`; sent `cull:` with the receiver.
    /// @answers the value of `block`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 1', for a block of two parameters or
    /// more.
    public ifNotNil: block = (
      ^block cull: self.
    )

    /// Evaluates `notNilBlock`, since the receiver is not nil, giving it
    /// the receiver when it takes an argument; `nilBlock` is not
    /// evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param nilBlock any object.
    /// @param notNilBlock as `ifNotNil:`'s block.
    /// @answers the value of `notNilBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 1', for a `notNilBlock` of two
    /// parameters or more.
    public ifNil: nilBlock ifNotNil: notNilBlock = (
      ^notNilBlock cull: self.
    )

    /// As `ifNil:ifNotNil:`, its arguments in the other order.
    ///
    /// @param notNilBlock as `ifNotNil:`'s block.
    /// @param nilBlock any object; not evaluated.
    /// @answers the value of `notNilBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 1', for a `notNilBlock` of two
    /// parameters or more.
    /// @see `ifNil:ifNotNil:`.
    public ifNotNil: notNilBlock ifNil: nilBlock = (
      ^notNilBlock cull: self.
    )

    /// Signals a new `Halt`; unhandled, it ends the run, unless an
    /// attached debugger's `Halt` breakpoint stops it there, which goes on
    /// resumed with nil.
    ///
    /// @answers the value a handler resumes the Halt with, or nil once
    /// the debugger's stop goes on.
    /// @signals Halt a new one, without a `messageText`.
    public halt = (
      ^outer Kernel Halt new signal.
    )
  )

  /// The superclass of `Class` and `Metaclass`: the equality and hash of
  /// every class object and every metaclass.
  ///
  /// §3.2: "Each class is an instance of a unique metaclass. All
  /// metaclasses are instances of class Metaclass, and are direct
  /// subclasses of Class." The specification names neither `Behavior`
  /// nor `Metaclass`'s superclass; this hierarchy keeps what only a real
  /// class has on `Class` so that a metaclass does not inherit it. A class
  /// object's state lives in the runtime (heap record); these three carry
  /// behaviour only. `Object` declares no accessor for any of them.
  public class Behavior = Object () (
    /// Compares the receiver with `other`: by identity, or, for a class
    /// object whose enclosing object is a value, by declaration,
    /// enclosing object and superclass. It never signals itself, though
    /// an enclosing object's own `=` may.
    ///
    /// A class object that is a value has no identity: two are equal
    /// when they apply one mixin application to equal enclosing objects
    /// over equal superclasses (docs/design/object-model.md, "Class
    /// sides and metaclasses"). The leaf answers every case it can
    /// decide without sending, identity included for a class object
    /// that is no value; it declines only where an enclosing object's
    /// class brings its own `=`. `==` answers what `=` does, as §3.1.1
    /// asks of a value's identity method, and forwards to the same leaf
    /// rather than sending `=`, so that comparing two class objects
    /// with `==` costs no activation.
    ///
    /// A nested class's class object is made per enclosing object (§6.1),
    /// so its equality follows its enclosing object's: two `Outer new
    /// Inner` of an ordinary `Outer` are unequal, while for a value class
    /// `Outer` whose factory takes `x:`, `(Outer x: 1) Inner = (Outer x:
    /// 1.0) Inner` is true, and two instances of those two classes whose
    /// slots are equal are equal too. Every class the kernel declares
    /// is a value, its enclosing object being the kernel. It keeps
    /// `Object>>=`'s contract, `hash` agreeing, wherever the enclosing
    /// objects' and the superclasses' own `=` keep it.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is the receiver or, for a class object
    /// whose enclosing object is a value, a class object of the same
    /// declaration whose enclosing object and superclass are equal to the
    /// receiver's, compared with `=`. A Boolean wherever the leaf decides;
    /// where it declines, what `behaviorEquals:` answers.
    /// @see `behaviorEquals:` and `Object>>=`.
    public = other = (
      ^vmMirror value: self sameAs: other ifFail: [^self behaviorEquals: other].
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` as `=` does: a value class
    /// object has no identity of its own, and any other class object's
    /// `=` is identity already.
    ///
    /// `Behavior` is no subclass of `Value`, so declaring `==` costs a
    /// class object nothing of §3.1.1: whether one is a value is decided
    /// by its enclosing object alone. For a class object that is no value
    /// it answers identity, as `Object>>==` would; for one that is a value
    /// it answers what `=` does, as §3.1.1 asks of a value's identity
    /// method, so that two equal class objects are identical -- the class
    /// of one value's instance is `==` to the class of an equal value's.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers what `=` answers.
    /// @see `=` and `Object>>==`.
    public == other = (
      ^vmMirror value: self sameAs: other ifFail: [^self behaviorEquals: other].
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver so that the hash agrees with `=`.
    ///
    /// A class object that is a value hashes as its name -- a metaclass as
    /// its class's name followed by ' class' -- since equal class objects
    /// apply one mixin and so share a name, and a name, unlike anything
    /// the runtime numbers, survives a copy and an image. Class objects of
    /// one declaration under unequal enclosing objects hash alike, which
    /// the contract allows. Any other class object, whose `=` is identity,
    /// answers its identity hash.
    ///
    /// @answers an Integer: the hash of the class's name, as a String's,
    /// for a class object that is a value; its identity hash, from 1 to
    /// 2^22 - 1, for any other.
    /// @see `Object>>hash`.
    public hash = (
      ^vmMirror valueHash: self ifFail: [^super hash].
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver as `hash` does, so that the identity hash
    /// agrees with `==`, which answers what `=` does: a value class
    /// object's identity is its equality, and any other's `hash` is its
    /// identity hash already. An `IdentityMap` therefore finds a value
    /// class object by any class object equal to it.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's `hash`.
    /// @see `Object>>identityHash`.
    public identityHash = (
      ^self hash.
    )

    /// Identifies the receiver as a Behavior, without a class test.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isKindOfBehavior = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// The equality the leaf of `=` leaves to Newspeak, comparing the
    /// states with `=`: the leaf's state, the enclosing object reflection
    /// reports and the one its level's code reaches, which differ only
    /// for the class object of a declaration without a body.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is a class object of the receiver's mixin
    /// application -- and of its declaration, for the class object of a
    /// declaration without a body -- whose enclosing objects and
    /// superclass equal the receiver's; false for an `other` that is no
    /// class object. Its last comparison is the superclasses' `=`, so the
    /// answer is not certainly a Boolean: a class side may declare an `=`
    /// of its own.
    private behaviorEquals: other = (
      (vmMirror behavior: self sharesApplicationWith: other ifFail: [^false]) ifFalse: [^false].
      ((self enclosingOf: self) = (self enclosingOf: other)) ifFalse: [^false].
      ((self levelEnclosingOf: self) = (self levelEnclosingOf: other)) ifFalse: [^false].
      ^(self superclassOf: self) = (self superclassOf: other).
    )

    /// Reads `subject`'s enclosing object, the one reflection reports,
    /// through the VM mirror.
    ///
    /// Both name their subject, as `Value`'s slot readers do: the other
    /// side's state has to be read through the same leaf as this
    /// side's.
    ///
    /// @param subject the receiver or the object it is compared with.
    /// @answers `subject`'s enclosing object: nil for a module's class,
    /// and nil for a `subject` that is no class object.
    private enclosingOf: subject = (
      ^vmMirror behaviorEnclosing: subject ifFail: [^nil].
    )

    /// Reads the enclosing object `subject`'s own level reaches, through
    /// the VM mirror; it differs from `enclosingOf:`'s only for the class
    /// object of a declaration without a body and for its metaclass.
    ///
    /// @param subject as `enclosingOf:`'s.
    /// @answers the one the class its last clause named has, for the class
    /// object of a declaration without a body and for its metaclass, whose
    /// `enclosingOf:` is the declaration's; `enclosingOf:`'s for every
    /// other class object and metaclass, and nil for a `subject` that is
    /// no class object.
    /// @see `enclosingOf:`.
    private levelEnclosingOf: subject = (
      ^vmMirror behaviorLevelEnclosing: subject ifFail: [^nil].
    )

    /// Reads `subject`'s superclass through the VM mirror.
    ///
    /// @param subject as `enclosingOf:`'s.
    /// @answers `subject`'s superclass, a class object: nil for `Top`
    /// and for a `subject` that is no class object.
    private superclassOf: subject = (
      ^vmMirror behaviorSuperclass: subject ifFail: [^nil].
    )
  )

  /// What a class object answers, as an instance of its metaclass: its
  /// name, its printed form and `handles:`.
  public class Class = Behavior () (
    /// Reads the class's name off its declaration, through the VM
    /// mirror.
    ///
    /// @answers the class's name as its declaration writes it, a new
    /// String on every send.
    public name = (
      ^vmMirror className: self ifFail: [^nil].
    )

    /// Prints the class as its name.
    ///
    /// @answers the class's `name`.
    public printString = (
      ^name.
    )

    /// Tests whether an `on:do:` for the receiver catches `exception`.
    /// `on:do:` asks it of its exception class. It never signals.
    ///
    /// ANSI's `handles:`, by default "an instance of me or of a
    /// subclass", compared by declaration so that a nested exception
    /// class catches its instances under any enclosing instance: a class
    /// declared without a body is its own declaration's class and the
    /// class of the mixin it applies, never another declaration's that
    /// applies the same mixin. Here rather
    /// than on `Exception`'s class side because §3.2's metaclasses are
    /// flat: a class side is not inherited, so only `Class` reaches every
    /// exception class, and a class side that declares its own
    /// `handles:` is searched first and overrides it
    /// (docs/design/core-library.md, "Exceptions").
    ///
    /// @param exception any object.
    /// @answers whether `exception` is an instance of the receiver or of
    /// a subclass of it, compared by declaration, so that an instance
    /// made under any enclosing object counts; a Boolean.
    public handles: exception = (
      ^vmMirror object: exception inheritsMixinOf: self ifFail: [false].
    )
  )

  /// The class of every metaclass: a metaclass's name, printed form and
  /// equality.
  public class Metaclass = Behavior () (
    /// Names the metaclass after its class.
    ///
    /// @answers a new String: its class's name followed by ' class'.
    public name = (
      ^thisClass name , ' class'.
    )

    /// Prints the metaclass as its name.
    ///
    /// @answers the metaclass's `name`.
    public printString = (
      ^name.
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by the classes they are the
    /// metaclasses of. It never signals itself, though the class's `=`
    /// may.
    ///
    /// A metaclass's enclosing object is its class's and its superclass
    /// is always `Class`, so its class is all its state: two metaclasses
    /// are equal exactly when their classes are, and keep `Object>>=`'s
    /// contract as their classes do. Its `hash` is `Behavior>>hash`, its
    /// class's name followed by ' class' for a metaclass that is a value,
    /// which equal metaclasses share. `==` is the same
    /// forwarder as `=`, as `Behavior`'s is, so that it answers what `=`
    /// answers (§3.1.1) and costs no activation.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is the metaclass of a class equal to the
    /// receiver's class. A Boolean wherever the leaf decides; where it
    /// declines, what the class's `=` answers, which a class side may
    /// declare for itself.
    /// @see `Behavior>>=` and `Object>>=`.
    public = other = (
      ^vmMirror value: self sameAs: other ifFail: [
        ^(self thisClassOf: self) = (self thisClassOf: other).
      ].
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` as `=` does, through the same
    /// leaf: identity for a metaclass that is no value, equality for one
    /// that is, as `Behavior>>==` answers for a class.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers what `=` answers.
    /// @see `=` and `Behavior>>==`.
    public == other = (
      ^vmMirror value: self sameAs: other ifFail: [
        ^(self thisClassOf: self) = (self thisClassOf: other).
      ].
    )

    /// Reads the class the receiver is the metaclass of.
    ///
    /// The class is the runtime's state, read through a leaf, not a
    /// slot.
    ///
    /// @answers the class the receiver is the metaclass of.
    private thisClass = (
      ^vmMirror metaclassThisClass: self ifFail: [^nil].
    )

    /// Reads the class `subject` is the metaclass of, through the same
    /// leaf as `thisClass`.
    ///
    /// @param subject the receiver or the object it is compared with.
    /// @answers the class `subject` is the metaclass of, nil for a
    /// `subject` that is no metaclass.
    private thisClassOf: subject = (
      ^vmMirror metaclassThisClass: subject ifFail: [^nil].
    )
  )

  /// The root of the exceptions: signalled with `signal` or `signal:`,
  /// caught by `Closure>>on:do:`, whose handler block receives it and may
  /// send it `resume:`, `return:`, `retry` or `pass`. Resumable.
  ///
  /// §8.6: exception handling is a library issue, built here over the
  /// activation (docs/design/core-library.md, "Exceptions"). The slots are
  /// the exception's own state; `originSelector` and `originReceiver` are
  /// set by the VM for an error it raises, so a host still receives the
  /// selector and receiver it received before exceptions existed.
  /// `searching` is true from `signal` or `pass` until a handler is
  /// invoked or the default action runs, and `reachedDefault` from then
  /// on, for good: the VM reads both to tell a search that overran the
  /// depth limit's reserve from a handler that did
  /// (`Mutator::overrun_of_the_reserve`).
  public class Exception = Object (
    |
      /// The activation of the `signal` that raised the exception, which
      /// `resume:` returns from; nil before `signal` and once a handler
      /// has left through `resume:`, `return:`, `retry` or by
      /// answering. A `^` out of the handler leaves it set.
      private signalActivation ::= nil.
      /// The `on:do:` activation whose handler runs, which `return:`
      /// and `retry` leave, and where the search for the next handler
      /// starts; the `signal` activation while a search starts, nil
      /// once a handler has left through `resume:`, `return:`, `retry`
      /// or by answering, or no handler was found. A `^` out of the
      /// handler leaves it set.
      private handlerActivation ::= nil.
      /// Whether a search has found no handler and run `defaultAction`;
      /// once true, it stays true.
      private reachedDefault ::= false.
      /// Whether a search for a handler is under way: true from
      /// `signal` or `pass` until a handler or `defaultAction` runs.
      private searching ::= false.
      /// The selector of the send the VM raised the exception for, nil
      /// for an exception a program signals itself.
      private originSelector ::= nil.
      /// The receiver of that send, set with `originSelector`.
      /// `hostReceiver` reads it only while `originSelector` is set, since
      /// the receiver of the send the VM raised for may itself be nil.
      private originReceiver ::= nil.
      /// The text describing the exception, nil until `signal:` or
      /// `messageText:` sets it; any object. `messageText:` sets it and
      /// answers the receiver.
      public messageText ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Describes the exception by its text, or by its class when it has
    /// none.
    ///
    /// @answers `messageText`, or the name of the receiver's class, a
    /// String, when `messageText` is nil.
    public description = (
      ^messageText isNil ifTrue: [self class name] ifFalse: [messageText].
    )

    /// Prints the exception as its `description`.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's `description`.
    public printString = (
      ^self description.
    )

    /// Tells whether a handler may `resume:` the exception, which it may
    /// here.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isResumable = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Tells whether a `signal` of the exception has found no handler
    /// and run `defaultAction`; once true, it stays true.
    ///
    /// @answers whether the default action was reached, a Boolean.
    public reachedDefaultHandler = (
      ^reachedDefault.
    )

    /// Signals the exception: offers it to each enabled `on:do:` on the
    /// stack, innermost first, whose exception class `handles:` it, and
    /// runs the handler of the first that does on top of the stack,
    /// with every `on:do:` passed disabled while it runs. A handler
    /// that returns instead of resuming makes its `on:do:` answer. With
    /// no handler, `defaultAction` runs; `Exception`'s ends the run.
    ///
    /// ANSI's signal: the handler runs on top of the stack, above this
    /// activation, and nothing is unwound until it decides.
    ///
    /// @answers the value the handler resumes the exception with; nothing
    /// to its sender when the handler returns instead. With no handler,
    /// what `defaultAction` answers, should a subclass's return.
    public signal = (
      signalActivation:: vmMirror currentActivation: self ifFail: [nil].
      handlerActivation:: signalActivation.
      searching:: true.
      ^self invokeNextHandler.
    )

    /// Sets `messageText` to `text` and signals the exception, as
    /// `signal` does.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers what `signal` answers.
    /// @see `signal`.
    public signal: text = (
      messageText:: text.
      ^self signal.
    )

    /// From a handler: offers the exception to the enabled `on:do:`s
    /// outside the handler's, innermost first, or runs `defaultAction`
    /// when none handles it.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender when a handler outside handles the
    /// exception: its `resume:` makes the `signal` that raised the
    /// exception answer, and its return makes its own `on:do:` answer.
    /// With none, what `defaultAction` answers, should a subclass's
    /// return.
    /// @signals Error 'pass sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', when no handler is running for the exception.
    public pass = (
      handlerActivation isNil ifTrue: [^self notInAHandler: #pass].
      searching:: true.
      ^self invokeNextHandler.
    )

    /// From a handler: ends the handler's `on:do:`, which answers
    /// `value`, running the pending `ensure:` and `ifCurtailed:` blocks
    /// on the way. Never answers to its sender.
    ///
    /// @param value any object.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: the handler's `on:do:` answers
    /// `value`.
    /// @signals Error 'return: sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', when no handler is running for the exception.
    /// @signals CannotReturn carrying `value`, 'cannotReturn: the
    /// activation has already returned', when its `on:do:` has returned
    /// already.
    public return: value = (
      |
        target = handlerActivation.
      |
      (target isNil or: [target == signalActivation]) ifTrue: [^self notInAHandler: #return:].
      signalActivation:: nil.
      handlerActivation:: nil.
      ^vmMirror activation: target returnValue: value ifFail: [nil].
    )

    /// As `return:`, with nil.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: the handler's `on:do:` answers
    /// nil.
    /// @signals Error 'return: sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', as `return:` does.
    /// @signals CannotReturn carrying nil, as `return:` does.
    /// @see `return:`.
    public return = (
      ^self return: nil.
    )

    /// From a handler: evaluates the handler's `on:do:` again from the
    /// start of its protected block, after the pending `ensure:` and
    /// `ifCurtailed:` blocks have run. Never answers to its sender.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: the `on:do:` answers what its
    /// protected block, evaluated again, makes it answer.
    /// @signals Error 'retry sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', when no handler is running for the exception.
    /// @signals CannotReturn 'cannotReturn: the activation has already
    /// returned', when its `on:do:` has returned already.
    public retry = (
      |
        target = handlerActivation.
      |
      (target isNil or: [target == signalActivation]) ifTrue: [^self notInAHandler: #retry].
      signalActivation:: nil.
      handlerActivation:: nil.
      ^vmMirror activationRestart: target ifFail: [nil].
    )

    /// From a handler: makes the `signal` that raised the exception
    /// answer `value`, leaving the handler and running the pending
    /// `ensure:` and `ifCurtailed:` blocks on the way. Never answers to
    /// its sender.
    ///
    /// @param value any object.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: the `signal` answers `value`.
    /// @signals Error 'cannot resume a non-resumable exception', when
    /// `isResumable` is false.
    /// @signals Error 'resume: sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', when no handler is running for the exception.
    /// @signals CannotReturn carrying `value`, 'cannotReturn: the
    /// activation has already returned', when the `signal` has returned
    /// already.
    public resume: value = (
      |
        target
      |
      self isResumable ifFalse: [
        ^(outer Kernel Error new) signal: 'cannot resume a non-resumable exception'.
      ].
      target:: signalActivation.
      target isNil ifTrue: [^self notInAHandler: #resume:].
      signalActivation:: nil.
      handlerActivation:: nil.
      ^vmMirror activation: target returnValue: value ifFail: [nil].
    )

    /// As `resume:`, with nil.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: the `signal` answers nil.
    /// @signals Error 'cannot resume a non-resumable exception', as
    /// `resume:` does.
    /// @signals Error 'resume: sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', as `resume:` does.
    /// @signals CannotReturn carrying nil, as `resume:` does.
    /// @see `resume:`.
    public resume = (
      ^self resume: nil.
    )

    /// What `signal` runs when no handler handles the exception: ends
    /// the run once the pending `ensure:` and `ifCurtailed:` blocks
    /// have run, reporting the `description` and, for an error the VM
    /// raised, the selector and receiver of the send that raised it.
    /// Never answers.
    ///
    /// No handler: the run ends through the leaf, once the pending
    /// `ensure:` blocks have run. An attached debugger stops at this
    /// method's push and lets it run when the program goes on
    /// (`invokeNextHandler`). The leaf takes any selector, receiver and
    /// description and declines only for a future, which the VM sees
    /// through before any failure block could run, so the failure block
    /// never runs and an unhandled exception cannot carry on.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing; it never returns.
    public defaultAction = (
      |
        selector = self hostSelector.
        receiver = self hostReceiver.
      |
      ^vmMirror terminate: self
          selector: selector
          receiver: receiver
          description: self description
          ifFail: [nil].
    )

    /// Chooses the selector `defaultAction` reports. A subclass overrides
    /// it to report another.
    ///
    /// The VM sets the origin as a pair, and its receiver may be nil,
    /// so whether there is one is read off the selector.
    ///
    /// @answers the selector the VM raised the exception for, else
    /// #signal.
    protected hostSelector = (
      ^originSelector isNil ifTrue: [#signal] ifFalse: [originSelector].
    )

    /// Chooses the receiver `defaultAction` reports. A subclass overrides
    /// it with `hostSelector`.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver of the send the VM raised the exception for,
    /// else the exception itself.
    /// @see `hostSelector`.
    protected hostReceiver = (
      ^originSelector isNil ifTrue: [self] ifFalse: [originReceiver].
    )

    /// The search `signal` and `pass` run: walks the senders from
    /// `handlerActivation` outwards and invokes the handler of the
    /// first enabled `on:do:` whose exception class `handles:` the
    /// exception, disabling every enabled `on:do:` passed on the way,
    /// the handler's included; with none, marks the default reached and
    /// runs `defaultAction` -- or, for a `Halt` an attached debugger's
    /// `Halt` breakpoint stopped, resumes it with nil.
    ///
    /// Each step is one `activationNextHandler:`, which answers the next
    /// enabled `on:do:` activation outward and reifies no activation it
    /// passes, so a signal allocates the same however deep it is raised
    /// below its handler.
    ///
    /// The search walks from where the last one stopped, counting every
    /// enabled `on:do:` it meets. When one handles this exception, it
    /// walks the same range again and collects the activations it
    /// counted, the handler last, into an array for
    /// `invokeHandlerAt:disabling:`. It cannot collect as it goes
    /// without a growable collection, and a walk after the disabling
    /// could not tell the handlers this search passed from ones
    /// disabled before it. The second walk is written out here rather
    /// than in a method of its own, so that a search pushes no
    /// activation it did not push before: the depth limit's reserve is
    /// counted in activations.
    ///
    /// @answers what `defaultAction` answers when no `on:do:` handles the
    /// exception; nothing when one does, whose handler ends through
    /// `invokeHandlerAt:disabling:`, nor for a `Halt` the debugger
    /// resumed, whose `signal` answers nil.
    /// @see `invokeHandlerAt:disabling:`.
    private invokeNextHandler = (
      |
        handlers
        index
        count ::= 0.
        activation ::= vmMirror activationNextHandler: handlerActivation ifFail: [nil].
      |
      [activation isNil] whileFalse: [
        count:: count + 1.
        (self isHandledAt: activation) ifTrue: [
          handlers:: outer Kernel Array new: count.
          index:: 0.
          activation:: vmMirror activationNextHandler: handlerActivation ifFail: [nil].
          [index < count] whileTrue: [
            index:: index + 1.
            handlers at: index put: activation.
            index < count ifTrue: [
              activation:: vmMirror activationNextHandler: activation ifFail: [nil].
            ].
          ].
          ^self invokeHandlerAt: (handlers at: count) disabling: handlers.
        ].
        activation:: vmMirror activationNextHandler: activation ifFail: [nil].
      ].
      reachedDefault:: true.
      searching:: false.
      handlerActivation:: nil.
      /// An attached debugger's exception breakpoints stop the program at
      /// the push of `defaultAction`, before any override of it runs, with
      /// the whole stack still there; with none attached the leaf does
      /// nothing. A `Halt` its breakpoint stopped goes on resumed with
      /// nil, as a debugger that let the user look goes on.
      (vmMirror exceptionUnhandled: self ifFail: [false]) ifTrue: [^self resume: nil].
      ^self defaultAction.
    )

    /// Tests whether the `on:do:` activation `activation` handles the
    /// exception, by sending its exception class `handles:` while that
    /// `on:do:` is disabled, and enabling it again however the test is
    /// left.
    ///
    /// The candidate is disabled while `handles:` runs, so that an
    /// exception `handles:` raises -- a `nil handles:`'s
    /// `doesNotUnderstand:` for `on: nil do:` -- is not offered to the
    /// very `on:do:` whose test raised it: that would test it again, and
    /// every nested search would walk the whole stack once more. It is
    /// enabled again however the test is left, as the handlers
    /// `invokeHandlerAt:disabling:` disables are: an `on:do:` inside the
    /// protected block may catch that exception and return, which leaves
    /// the candidate on the stack.
    ///
    /// @param activation an enabled `on:do:` activation on the stack.
    /// @answers what its exception class answers to `handles:`: a Boolean
    /// for `Class`'s `handles:`, though a class side may declare its own.
    private isHandledAt: activation = (
      |
        exceptionClass = vmMirror activationHandlerClass: activation ifFail: [nil].
      |
      vmMirror activation: activation handlerActive: false ifFail: [nil].
      ^[exceptionClass handles: self] ensure: [
        vmMirror activation: activation handlerActive: true ifFail: [nil].
      ].
    )

    /// Runs the handler block of the `on:do:` activation `activation`,
    /// sent `cull:` with the exception, while every activation of
    /// `handlers` is disabled, and ends that `on:do:` with the
    /// handler's value as `return:` does. The handlers are enabled
    /// again however the handler is left.
    ///
    /// ANSI's handler environment: an exception signalled while a handler
    /// runs is looked for outside the `on:do:` that caught this one --
    /// not in it, and not in any `on:do:` between it and the signal, the
    /// protected block's own. Every `on:do:` the search passed is
    /// disabled while the handler runs, rather than the search skipping
    /// from a handled exception's `signal` to its handler: that needs no
    /// way to recognize another exception's `signal` activation and
    /// costs the search's own walk again. They are enabled again in an
    /// `ensure:`, however the handler is left: returning, `resume:`,
    /// `retry`, `return:`, or a `^` out of it. The handler's value is an
    /// implicit `return:`.
    ///
    /// @param activation the `on:do:` whose class handles the exception.
    /// @param handlers an Array of the `on:do:` activations the search
    /// passed, `activation` last.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: the handler's value ends the
    /// `on:do:` through `return:`, and a handler that resumes, retries,
    /// returns or leaves by a `^` leaves past it. The exception: a handler
    /// that sent `pass` with no handler outside, whose `pass` a subclass's
    /// `defaultAction` let answer, has no handler running once it
    /// completes, so its `return:` signals instead.
    /// @signals Error 'return: sent to an exception that is not being
    /// handled', in that case: the search that found no handler cleared
    /// the handler activation.
    /// @see `return:`.
    private invokeHandlerAt: activation disabling: handlers = (
      |
        result
      |
      handlerActivation:: activation.
      handlers do: [:each | vmMirror activation: each handlerActive: false ifFail: [nil]].
      result:: [
        searching:: false.
        /// The handler, read off `activation`, is about to run on top of
        /// the stack, before anything unwinds: an attached debugger's
        /// caught-exception breakpoint stops at the handler's first
        /// instruction, and a step whose exception this handler takes
        /// goes on into it; with none attached the row only answers the
        /// handler. Reading it through the exit row that reports the catch
        /// spares the search a send for the report.
        (vmMirror exceptionCaught: self at: activation ifFail: [nil]) cull: self.
      ] ensure: [
        handlers do: [:each | vmMirror activation: each handlerActive: true ifFail: [nil]].
      ].
      ^self return: result.
    )

    /// Refuses a handler-only message sent outside a handler. It never
    /// returns: an Error is not resumable.
    ///
    /// @param selector the Symbol of the message sent.
    /// @answers nothing; it never returns.
    /// @signals Error '… sent to an exception that is not being handled',
    /// naming `selector`.
    private notInAHandler: selector = (
      ^(outer Kernel Error new) signal: selector asString
          , ' sent to an exception that is not being handled'.
    )
  ) : (
    /// Signals a new instance of the receiver, as `Exception>>signal`
    /// does.
    ///
    /// §3.2's metaclasses are flat, so each kernel exception class
    /// declares these on its own class side; `handles:` is `Class`'s.
    ///
    /// @answers what the new exception's `signal` answers: the value a
    /// handler resumes it with; nothing to its sender when the handler
    /// returns instead.
    /// @signals Exception a new one, without a `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// Signals a new instance of the receiver with `messageText` set to
    /// `text`, as `Exception>>signal:` does.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers what the new exception's `signal` answers: the value a
    /// handler resumes it with; nothing to its sender when the handler
    /// returns instead.
    /// @signals Exception a new one carrying `text`.
    /// @see `Exception>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// An exception that is not resumable; the VM's own errors are Errors.
  /// A handler ends one by returning, `return:`, `retry` or a `^`, or
  /// passes it on; its `resume:` signals an Error of its own.
  public class Error = Exception () (
    /// Tells whether a handler may `resume:` the exception, which it may
    /// not: an Error's `resume:` signals an Error of its own.
    ///
    /// @answers false: a handler cannot `resume:` an Error.
    public isResumable = (
      ^false.
    )
  ) : (
    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new Error.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: no handler can resume an Error, so
    /// a handler's `on:do:` answers instead, and with no handler the run
    /// ends.
    /// @signals Error a new one, without a `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new Error with `text`.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender, as `signal`.
    /// @signals Error a new one carrying `text`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// What `Object>>doesNotUnderstand:` signals for a send nothing
  /// understood, with the text 'an object does not understand #…' naming
  /// the selector. A handler's `resume:` makes the failed send answer the
  /// value it resumes with.
  ///
  /// §5.5's exception. Resumable although an `Error`, so that the
  /// debugger can create the missing method and carry on:
  /// `Object>>doesNotUnderstand:` then sends the message again. A host
  /// receives the selector and receiver of the send nothing understood.
  public class MessageNotUnderstood = Error (
    |
      /// The `Message` nothing understood, nil for one a program
      /// signals itself; `message:` sets it and answers the
      /// receiver.
      public message ::= nil.
      /// The object the message was sent to; `receiver:` sets it and
      /// answers the exception.
      public receiver ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Tells whether a handler may `resume:` the exception, which it may,
    /// although it is an Error.
    ///
    /// @answers true: a handler may `resume:` it.
    public isResumable = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Chooses the selector `defaultAction` reports: the one nothing
    /// understood.
    ///
    /// One a program signals itself may carry no message: it then ends
    /// the run as any other exception does.
    ///
    /// @answers the selector of `message`, or `Exception`'s when there is
    /// no message.
    /// @see `Exception>>hostSelector`.
    protected hostSelector = (
      ^message isNil ifTrue: [super hostSelector] ifFalse: [message selector].
    )

    /// Chooses the receiver `defaultAction` reports: the object the
    /// message nothing understood was sent to.
    ///
    /// @answers `receiver`, or `Exception`'s when there is no message.
    /// @see `Exception>>hostReceiver`.
    protected hostReceiver = (
      ^message isNil ifTrue: [super hostReceiver] ifFalse: [receiver].
    )
  ) : (
    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new MessageNotUnderstood,
    /// which carries no message.
    ///
    /// @answers what the new exception's `signal` answers: the value a
    /// handler resumes it with; nothing to its sender when the handler
    /// returns instead.
    /// @signals MessageNotUnderstood a new one, without a message or a
    /// `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new
    /// MessageNotUnderstood with `text`, which carries no message.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers what the new exception's `signal` answers: the value a
    /// handler resumes it with; nothing to its sender when the handler
    /// returns instead.
    /// @signals MessageNotUnderstood a new one carrying `text` and no
    /// message.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// What `Object>>halt` signals: a resumable exception which, unhandled,
  /// ends the run.
  public class Halt = Exception () () : (
    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new Halt.
    ///
    /// @answers what the new exception's `signal` answers: the value a
    /// handler resumes it with; nothing to its sender when the handler
    /// returns instead.
    /// @signals Halt a new one, without a `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new Halt with `text`.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers what the new exception's `signal` answers: the value a
    /// handler resumes it with; nothing to its sender when the handler
    /// returns instead.
    /// @signals Halt a new one carrying `text`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// An argument a method cannot take: an index out of range, a size, an
  /// object of the wrong kind, a divisor of zero, a block given the wrong
  /// number of arguments. Carries the argument.
  public class ArgumentError = Error (
    |
      /// The argument that was refused, any object; `argument:` sets it
      /// and answers the receiver.
      ///
      /// Named `argument`, not `value`: an exception answering `value`
      /// would pass for a block wherever the kernel sends `value` to an
      /// argument.
      public argument ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Describes the error by its text, or by the refused argument when
    /// it has none.
    ///
    /// @answers `messageText`, or a new String of 'ArgumentError: '
    /// followed by the argument's `printString` when it is nil.
    public description = (
      ^messageText isNil ifTrue: ['ArgumentError: ' , argument printString] ifFalse: [messageText].
    )
  ) : (
    /// Makes the error for `offending`, not yet signalled.
    ///
    /// @param offending the refused argument, any object.
    /// @answers a new ArgumentError carrying `offending`.
    public value: offending = (
      ^self new argument: offending.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new ArgumentError, which
    /// carries nil.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: no handler can resume an Error, so
    /// a handler's `on:do:` answers instead, and with no handler the run
    /// ends.
    /// @signals ArgumentError a new one, carrying nil, without a
    /// `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new ArgumentError with
    /// `text`, which carries nil.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender, as `signal`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError a new one carrying `text` and nil.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// A return to an activation that has already returned: a `^` from a
  /// block whose home has returned, or a `return:`, `retry` or `resume:`
  /// aimed at one. Carries the value that was being returned.
  public class CannotReturn = Error (
    |
      /// The value that could not be returned, any object; `result:`
      /// sets it and answers the receiver.
      public result ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Describes the error by its text, or by the value that could not
    /// be returned when it has none.
    ///
    /// @answers `messageText`, or a new String of 'CannotReturn: '
    /// followed by the result's `printString` when it is nil.
    public description = (
      ^messageText isNil ifTrue: ['CannotReturn: ' , result printString] ifFalse: [messageText].
    )
  ) : (
    /// Makes the error for `value`, not yet signalled.
    ///
    /// @param value the value that could not be returned, any object.
    /// @answers a new CannotReturn carrying `value`.
    public result: value = (
      ^self new result: value.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new CannotReturn, which
    /// carries nil.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: no handler can resume an Error, so
    /// a handler's `on:do:` answers instead, and with no handler the run
    /// ends.
    /// @signals CannotReturn a new one, carrying nil, without a
    /// `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new CannotReturn with
    /// `text`, which carries nil.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender, as `signal`.
    /// @signals CannotReturn a new one carrying `text` and nil.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// What a `whileTrue:` or `whileFalse:` signals when its condition
  /// answers an object that is no Boolean. Carries that object.
  ///
  /// Private, so a program catches it as an `Error`: no program needs to
  /// name the one mistake it reports.
  private class NonBooleanReceiver = Error (
    |
      /// The condition's answer, any object; `receiver:` sets it and
      /// answers the exception.
      public receiver ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Describes the error by its text, or by the condition's answer
    /// when it has none.
    ///
    /// @answers `messageText`, or a new String of 'NonBooleanReceiver: '
    /// followed by the receiver's `printString` when it is nil.
    public description = (
      ^messageText isNil ifTrue: [
        'NonBooleanReceiver: ' , receiver printString.
      ] ifFalse: [
        messageText.
      ].
    )
  ) : (
    /// Makes the error for `value`, not yet signalled.
    ///
    /// @param value the condition's answer, any object.
    /// @answers a new NonBooleanReceiver carrying `value`.
    public receiver: value = (
      ^self new receiver: value.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new NonBooleanReceiver,
    /// which carries nil.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: no handler can resume an Error, so
    /// a handler's `on:do:` answers instead, and with no handler the run
    /// ends.
    /// @signals NonBooleanReceiver a new one, carrying nil, without a
    /// `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new NonBooleanReceiver
    /// with `text`, which carries nil.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender, as `signal`.
    /// @signals NonBooleanReceiver a new one carrying `text` and nil.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// A class that refused to make an instance through the factory it was
  /// sent: one made only by a literal, by its declaration, or by another
  /// factory. Carries the class, or nil when it may not be named.
  public class CannotInstantiate = Error (
    |
      /// The class that refused, or nil; `behavior:` sets it and
      /// answers the receiver.
      ///
      /// Named `behavior`, not `class`, which would override
      /// `Object>>class`.
      public behavior ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Describes the error by its text, or by the refusing class when it
    /// has none.
    ///
    /// @answers `messageText`, or a new String of 'CannotInstantiate: '
    /// followed by the refusing class's `printString` when it is nil.
    public description = (
      ^messageText isNil ifTrue: [
        'CannotInstantiate: ' , behavior printString.
      ] ifFalse: [
        messageText.
      ].
    )
  ) : (
    /// Makes the error for `refusing`, not yet signalled.
    ///
    /// @param refusing the class that refused, or nil.
    /// @answers a new CannotInstantiate carrying `refusing`.
    public behavior: refusing = (
      ^self new behavior: refusing.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal`: signals a new CannotInstantiate,
    /// which carries nil.
    ///
    /// @answers nothing to its sender: no handler can resume an Error, so
    /// a handler's `on:do:` answers instead, and with no handler the run
    /// ends.
    /// @signals CannotInstantiate a new one, carrying nil, without a
    /// `messageText`.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal`.
    public signal = (
      ^self new signal.
    )

    /// As `Exception class>>signal:`: signals a new CannotInstantiate
    /// with `text`, which carries nil.
    ///
    /// @param text the description, usually a String; stored as it is.
    /// @answers nothing to its sender, as `signal`.
    /// @signals CannotInstantiate a new one carrying `text` and nil.
    /// @see `Exception class>>signal:`.
    public signal: text = (
      ^self new signal: text.
    )
  )

  /// A message: a selector and its arguments. `doesNotUnderstand:`
  /// receives one, and a program may build one and send it.
  ///
  /// What a `doesNotUnderstand:` handler receives. `sendTo:` sends it as
  /// an ordinary public send (§5.5): a program can build a message, so a
  /// send through one must reach no protected or private method.
  public class Message = Object (
    |
      /// The selector, a Symbol; `selector:` sets it and answers the
      /// receiver.
      public selector ::= nil.
      /// The arguments, an Array, or nil for none; `arguments:` sets
      /// them and answers the receiver. The message reads the Array
      /// when it is sent, not when it is set.
      public arguments ::= nil.
    |
  ) (
    /// Sends the message to `receiver` as a public send: only public
    /// methods are found, and a selector nothing public answers reaches
    /// the receiver's `doesNotUnderstand:`. For a slot's accessor or a
    /// method backed by a native operation the number of arguments is not
    /// checked.
    ///
    /// A message built without `arguments:` holds nil there, and is
    /// sent with none.
    ///
    /// @param receiver any object.
    /// @answers the result of the send.
    /// @signals Error 'sendTo: needs a Symbol selector and an Array of
    /// arguments', when `selector` is no Symbol or `arguments` is neither
    /// an Array nor nil.
    /// @signals Error 'wrong number of arguments: `…` takes …, got …',
    /// when the method found is written in Newspeak and takes another
    /// number of arguments than `arguments` holds.
    public sendTo: receiver = (
      ^vmMirror message: self sendTo: receiver ifFail: [
        ^(outer
            Kernel
            Error
            new) signal: 'sendTo: needs a Symbol selector and an Array of arguments'.
      ].
    )
  )

  /// A running activation as an object: the frame it wraps, and nothing a
  /// program can reach -- it reads other frames and unwinds stacks, a
  /// capability Newspeak hands out only through mirrors.
  private class Activation = Object () ()

  /// One mixin as an object: an instance stands for exactly one mixin --
  /// a class's instance side or its class side -- and is the reflectee
  /// of the mixin mirror on it. It answers nothing of its own; only the
  /// mirrors read it, through the VM mirror.
  ///
  /// The runtime makes the instances, one per mixin, the same instance
  /// on every request, so `==` is the mixin's identity; `new` is
  /// refused. Private, so that no program can name it.
  private class Mixin = Object () ()

  /// An object that answers none of `Object`'s public messages itself:
  /// each is overridden as protected, so a message from outside reaches
  /// `doesNotUnderstand:`. A subclass that implements only
  /// `doesNotUnderstand:` forwards every message it is sent.
  ///
  /// §5.5's proxy, "Otherwise it would be possible to distinguish
  /// between an object and a proxy": lookupPublic stops at a protected
  /// method, and the failing send falls to `doesNotUnderstand:`. Inside
  /// the proxy, a self send still reaches each of them. A member added to
  /// `Object` without its override here fails
  /// `a_proxy_overrides_every_public_member_of_object_as_protected`.
  /// `class` and the class accessors need none: `Object` already
  /// declares them protected, and a bare `Integer` or `self class` inside
  /// a proxy keeps the accessor's direct answer.
  public class Proxy = Object () (
    /// As `Object>>==`, for a send from inside the proxy only: `self ==
    /// other` there is the proxy's own identity.
    ///
    /// Protected, so that a proxy can stand for its target: a `==` from
    /// outside reaches `doesNotUnderstand:`, and what it answers is the
    /// subclass's. A bare `Proxy` signals MessageNotUnderstood. A subclass
    /// that forwards every message sends `==` on to its target, so the
    /// holder of a proxy sees the target's identity from the proxy's side
    /// only: `proxy == target` is true, but `target == proxy` is false
    /// for every target whose `==` is the kernel's, which sees the proxy
    /// as the other object it is, and so is `proxy == proxy`, which is
    /// `target == proxy` -- such a proxy is not identical to itself from
    /// outside, and keeps neither the symmetry nor the reflexivity of
    /// `Object>>==`. A future is the exception:
    /// the runtime sees through it on either side, so a future is
    /// identical to its value and to itself.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is the receiver itself, a Boolean.
    /// @see `Object>>==`.
    protected == other = (
      ^super == other.
    )

    /// As `Object>>=`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// Protected for the reason `==` is. A forwarding subclass sends `=`
    /// on to its target, so `proxy = x` answers what `target = x` does,
    /// while `x = proxy` is `x`'s own comparison with an object of
    /// another class, which every `=` of the kernel answers false: for a
    /// target of the kernel's classes or of a class without an `=` of its
    /// own the equality is not symmetric, and `proxy = proxy` is false. A
    /// forwarding proxy stored as a key of a `Map` or an element of a
    /// `Set` is then never found again, by itself or by its target. A
    /// future is seen through on either side, as for `==`.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is the receiver itself, a Boolean.
    /// @see `Object>>=`.
    protected = other = (
      ^super = other.
    )

    /// As `Object>>hash`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// Protected for the reason `==` is: a forwarding subclass answers
    /// its target's `hash` from outside, so a proxy and its target hash
    /// alike, as the proxy's `=` with its target demands.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's identity hash, an Integer.
    /// @see `Object>>hash`.
    protected hash = (
      ^super hash.
    )

    /// As `Object>>yourself`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    /// @see `Object>>yourself`.
    protected yourself = (
      ^super yourself.
    )

    /// As `Object>>identityHash`, for a send from inside the proxy
    /// only: `self identityHash` there is the proxy's own.
    ///
    /// Protected for the reason `==` is: a forwarding subclass answers its
    /// target's identity hash from outside, so a proxy and its target
    /// share one, as `proxy == target` demands. An `IdentityMap` holding
    /// the target finds it by the proxy; one holding a forwarding proxy
    /// does not find it again wherever its `==` with itself is false.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's identity hash, an Integer.
    /// @see `Object>>identityHash`.
    protected identityHash = (
      ^super identityHash.
    )

    /// As `Object>>printString`, for a send from inside the proxy
    /// only.
    ///
    /// @answers the String 'an Object', a new String on every send.
    /// @see `Object>>printString`.
    protected printString = (
      ^super printString.
    )

    /// As `Object>>isNil`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// @answers false: the receiver is not nil.
    /// @see `Object>>isNil`.
    protected isNil = (
      ^super isNil.
    )

    /// As `Object>>notNil`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// @answers true: the receiver is not nil.
    /// @see `Object>>notNil`.
    protected notNil = (
      ^super notNil.
    )

    /// As `Object>>ifNil:`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// @param block any object; not evaluated.
    /// @answers the receiver.
    /// @see `Object>>ifNil:`.
    protected ifNil: block = (
      ^super ifNil: block.
    )

    /// As `Object>>ifNotNil:`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// @param block as `Object>>ifNotNil:`'s, sent `cull:` with the
    /// receiver.
    /// @answers the value of `block`.
    /// @see `Object>>ifNotNil:`.
    protected ifNotNil: block = (
      ^super ifNotNil: block.
    )

    /// As `Object>>ifNil:ifNotNil:`, for a send from inside the proxy
    /// only.
    ///
    /// @param nilBlock any object; not evaluated.
    /// @param notNilBlock as `Object>>ifNotNil:`'s block.
    /// @answers the value of `notNilBlock`.
    /// @see `Object>>ifNil:ifNotNil:`.
    protected ifNil: nilBlock ifNotNil: notNilBlock = (
      ^super ifNil: nilBlock ifNotNil: notNilBlock.
    )

    /// As `Object>>ifNotNil:ifNil:`, for a send from inside the proxy
    /// only.
    ///
    /// @param notNilBlock as `Object>>ifNotNil:`'s block.
    /// @param nilBlock any object; not evaluated.
    /// @answers the value of `notNilBlock`.
    /// @see `Object>>ifNotNil:ifNil:`.
    protected ifNotNil: notNilBlock ifNil: nilBlock = (
      ^super ifNotNil: notNilBlock ifNil: nilBlock.
    )

    /// As `Object>>halt`, for a send from inside the proxy only.
    ///
    /// @answers the value a handler resumes the Halt with.
    /// @signals Halt a new one, without a `messageText`.
    /// @see `Object>>halt`.
    protected halt = (
      ^super halt.
    )
  )

  /// A future: what `Future computing:` answers and what a simultaneous
  /// slot clause stores in a slot until the clause's end. Every message a
  /// program sends it forces it -- runs its computation once, the first
  /// time -- and is then sent on to the value. No program can name the
  /// class.
  ///
  /// §6.3.2's pull future: what a simultaneous slot clause stores in a slot
  /// until the clause's end resolves it (docs/design/object-model.md,
  /// "Simultaneous slots"), and what `Future computing:` answers. Private,
  /// as `Activation` is, and no program names it: as a `Proxy`, every
  /// message a program sends it reaches `doesNotUnderstand:`, which
  /// forwards it to the forced value -- §5.5's proxy, indistinguishable
  /// from its target. Forcing is `resolve`'s, an ordinary method, so that
  /// machine code runs it as it runs any other: only a `resolve` writes
  /// the two slots after the factory -- the computation, until it is
  /// resolved, and then the value, and the state, 0 unresolved, 1
  /// resolving, 2 resolved -- its own future's through the slots, and
  /// those of the futures it takes over through the VM mirror. The
  /// runtime reads them, by position, to see through a resolved future,
  /// and wherever it meets one that is not, it sends the future `resolve`
  /// (`interpreter/future.rs`). A simultaneous slot
  /// clause makes its futures in the runtime, of a block; `Future
  /// computing:` sends the factory, which stores the computation as it
  /// is, so a future handed to it is not forced until this one is. The
  /// initializer tells the runtime a future exists, which is what turns
  /// on its seeing through futures.
  ///
  /// @factory makes an unresolved future of `theComputation`, which it
  /// stores as it is, forcing nothing, and tells the runtime a future
  /// exists; `Future computing:` sends it.
  /// @param theComputation a block of no argument, which a forcing
  /// evaluates, or any other object, which a forcing sends `value`.
  private class FutureImplementation computing: theComputation = Proxy (
    |
      /// The computation until the future is resolved, then its value;
      /// written only by a `resolve` after the factory.
      private computation ::= theComputation.
      /// The state, written only by a `resolve`: 0 unresolved, 1
      /// resolving, 2 resolved.
      private state ::= 0.
    |
    vmMirror futureMade: self ifFail: [nil].
  ) (
    /// Forces the future and sends `message` to its value as a public
    /// send.
    ///
    /// @param message the `Message` the future was sent.
    /// @answers what that send answers.
    /// @signals Error every one `resolve` signals, before `message` is
    /// sent.
    /// @see `resolve`.
    protected doesNotUnderstand: message = (
      ^message sendTo: self resolve.
    )

    /// Forces the future, computing its value first when it is
    /// unresolved. The computation runs at most once per forcing that
    /// completes: a block is evaluated, any other object is sent
    /// `value`. When the computation answers a future, that future's
    /// value is this one's -- §6.3.2's pipelined promise -- so that the
    /// value answered and recorded is never a future: a resolved one's
    /// value is taken as it is, and an unresolved one is taken over --
    /// marked resolving, and its computation run here in its stead --
    /// and so on along a chain of futures each answering the next, which
    /// this one activation resolves at a depth that does not grow with
    /// the chain. Every future taken over is resolved to the same value,
    /// before this one. From then on each is resolved, and answers its
    /// value without running anything.
    ///
    /// A forcing an unwind abandons -- a computation signalled, a handler
    /// returned past it, the depth limit refused one of its activations
    /// -- leaves the future, and every future it took over, unresolved
    /// again, their computations kept, so that the next message computes
    /// them afresh rather than meeting a stale resolving state
    /// (docs/design/object-model.md, "A clause abandoned by an
    /// exception"). Each mark of resolving is set inside the protected
    /// block, and a future taken over is recorded before it is marked, so
    /// that no activation the forcing pushes can be refused between a mark
    /// and the `ifCurtailed:` that undoes it. The states are read through
    /// the VM mirror, which answers them as small integers, so that a
    /// state crafted code stored is compared by nothing it defines.
    ///
    /// @answers the future's value, never a future.
    /// @signals Error 'divergent evaluation: a future was forced while it
    /// was being computed', when the future, or one the chain reaches, is
    /// forced while it is being computed -- §6.3.2 resolves only
    /// well-founded recursion.
    /// @signals Error 'a malformed future: its fields are no future''s',
    /// when its slots hold what no forcing wrote: a state none of the
    /// three, or a value that is itself a future.
    /// @signals Error every one a computation signals, which passes
    /// through.
    private resolve = (
      |
        own = vmMirror futureState: self ifFail: [3].
        taken ::= nil.
        link ::= nil.
        inner ::= nil.
        value ::= nil.
      |
      own = 2 ifTrue: [^computation].
      own = 0 ifFalse: [^self signalForcingInState: own].
      ^[
        state:: 1.
        value:: computation value.
        [vmMirror objectIsFuture: value ifFail: [false]] whileTrue: [
          inner:: vmMirror futureState: value ifFail: [3].
          inner = 2 ifTrue: [
            value:: vmMirror futureComputation: value ifFail: [nil].
          ] ifFalse: [
            inner = 0 ifFalse: [self signalForcingInState: inner].
            taken:: {value. taken}.
            value:: (vmMirror futureTakeOver: value ifFail: [
              self signalForcingInState: (vmMirror futureState: value ifFail: [3]).
            ]) value.
          ].
        ].
        link:: taken.
        [link isNil] whileFalse: [
          vmMirror future: (link at: 1) resolveTo: value ifFail: [nil].
          link:: link at: 2.
        ].
        computation:: value.
        state:: 2.
        value.
      ] ifCurtailed: [
        state:: 0.
        [taken isNil] whileFalse: [
          vmMirror futureAbandon: (taken at: 1) ifFail: [nil].
          taken:: taken at: 2.
        ].
      ].
    )

    /// Signals the Error of forcing a future whose state, as
    /// `futureState:ifFail:` answers it, is `futureState`. Never answers:
    /// an Error is not resumable.
    ///
    /// @param futureState a state that is neither 0, unresolved, nor 2,
    /// resolved.
    /// @answers nothing; it never returns.
    /// @signals Error 'divergent evaluation: a future was forced while it
    /// was being computed' for 1, resolving.
    /// @signals Error 'a malformed future: its fields are no future''s'
    /// for any other.
    private signalForcingInState: futureState = (
      futureState = 1 ifTrue: [
        ^(outer Kernel Error new) signal: 'divergent evaluation: a future was forced '
            , 'while it was being computed'.
      ].
      ^(outer Kernel Error new) signal: 'a malformed future: its fields are no future''s'.
    )
  )

  /// The class whose `computing:` answers a future of a block: an object
  /// that runs the block on its first message and behaves as the block's
  /// value from then on. `Future new` signals a `CannotInstantiate`.
  ///
  /// A class only for its class side: the future it answers is a
  /// `FutureImplementation`, the one class the runtime forces and sees
  /// through, which no program can name or subclass, so every future
  /// keeps the rules of docs/design/object-model.md, "Simultaneous
  /// slots". Its initializer refuses, so a subclass's instances are
  /// refused too; a subclass does not inherit `computing:` (§3.2).
  ///
  /// @factory makes nothing: `new`, and every subclass's factory, signal
  /// before the instance is answered.
  /// @signals CannotInstantiate carrying the class instantiated, 'a Future
  /// is made by Future computing:', for every instantiation.
  public class Future = Object (
    (outer Kernel CannotInstantiate behavior: self
        class) signal: 'a Future is made by Future computing:'.
  ) () : (
    /// Makes a future of `computation`'s value, computed when the future
    /// is first sent a message.
    ///
    /// @param computation a block of no argument, which the future then
    /// runs, or any other object, which it then sends `value`. A future
    /// handed in is forced only then.
    /// @answers a new future, unresolved.
    public computing: computation = (
      ^outer Kernel FutureImplementation computing: computation.
    )
  )

  /// The superclass of every value class: its instances are deeply
  /// immutable, two are equal when their classes are equal and their
  /// slots are equal, and a value has no identity of its own -- its `==`
  /// is its `=` and its `identityHash` its `hash`.
  ///
  /// §3.1.1: an object is a value object -- deeply immutable and globally
  /// unique -- only if "its class inherits from class `Value`", so every
  /// class whose instances are values hangs below this one.
  ///
  /// An instance becomes a value when its factory returns: §3.1.1 also asks
  /// that its slots "have been initialized", and until they all are it is
  /// under construction. While it is, it answers no question whose answer
  /// depends on its state: this class's own `=` and `hash`, the derived
  /// ones, and through them `==` and `identityHash`, signal rather than
  /// answer from the slots built so far, and so does asking whether it is
  /// a value, as `Ephemeron>>key:` and `WeakMap>>at:put:` do; a subclass
  /// that declares an `=` or a `hash` of its own decides that question for
  /// itself. A question whose answer is fixed whatever its state is
  /// answered: compared by the derived `=` as the argument of an object of
  /// a class it cannot be equal to -- `nil = aPoint`, `3 = aPoint` -- it is
  /// unequal to it. It may still be stored, in a slot of another
  /// value among other places, and be the enclosing object of the classes
  /// nested in it. A factory cut short -- by an unwind past it, or a signal
  /// its initializer does not handle -- never returns, and an instance it
  /// handed out stays under construction for good.
  ///
  /// "Globally unique, in the sense that no other object is equal to it":
  /// a value is its state, so its equality is derived from its class and
  /// its slots, never from which object holds them. §3.1.1 lets a value be
  /// copied freely across an actor boundary or shared, as the
  /// implementation chooses, so no program may tell a copy from its
  /// original: the copy is equal to it, identical to it by `==`, and has
  /// to hash like it -- which an identity hash, the copy's own, would not.
  /// Hence `==` sends `=` and `identityHash` answers `hash`, and two values
  /// that are equal are, to a program, one value: `'abc' == ('abcd'
  /// copyFrom: 1 to: 3)` and `1 == 1.0` are true, and an `IdentityMap`
  /// keeps equal values as one key. Only reflection still sees two
  /// objects -- §3.1.1: "Values are only immutable at the base level",
  /// and §3.7 isolates actors "modulo reflection" -- as an object mirror
  /// compares its reflectee as the object it is.
  ///
  /// A subclass may declare an `=` and a `hash` of its own -- the numbers,
  /// `String` and `Symbol` do, `True` and `False` declare `hash` -- and
  /// must then keep `Object>>=`'s contract, the two agreeing; its `==` and
  /// `identityHash` follow without being declared again. A subclass that
  /// declares `=` without `hash` keeps this `hash`, which then hashes its
  /// slots with their own `hash`, and is right only where its `=` compares
  /// exactly those slots. A subclass that declares `==` is no value class
  /// (§3.1.1), and the runtime refuses to instantiate it.
  ///
  /// Each class below it already declares the `hash` its own `=` demands:
  /// whenever `x = y` is true, `x hash = y hash` must be too. Since a
  /// value may be copied across an actor boundary, a copy has to hash
  /// like its original, so none of them may inherit `Object`'s identity
  /// hash. `Integer`'s, `True`'s and `False`'s are no forwarders to a
  /// leaf: the value they answer is already at hand.
  public class Value = Object () (
    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by class and slots: a value has
    /// no identity of its own. It signals itself only for an instance
    /// still under construction, though a slot's own `=` may; a subclass
    /// that declares its own `=` replaces this one, refusal included.
    ///
    /// §3.1.1: a value object is globally unique, in the sense that no
    /// other object is equal to it -- so equality is derived from state,
    /// and a value has no observable identity. The leaf declines when a
    /// slot's class brings its own `=`, because a leaf may not send;
    /// `valueEquals:` then sends it.
    ///
    /// The classes are compared first, with `=`: two instances of one
    /// class, or of two class objects equal by `Behavior>>=` -- one value's
    /// nested class under two equal enclosing values -- and nothing else,
    /// so a value never equals an object of another class, nor `nil`. Then
    /// the slots, in declaration order, each pair with the slot's own `=`:
    /// `3` and `3.0` in one slot are equal, as they are anywhere. It keeps
    /// `Object>>=`'s contract wherever the slots' `=` keep it, and `hash`
    /// agrees. The numbers, Strings and Symbols decide their own `=`;
    /// `true`, `false` and `nil` are compared here, each equal only to
    /// itself. A receiver that holds no number although its class is a
    /// number's -- `Fraction new`, an instance of a program's subclass of
    /// `Number` -- lands here when its own `=` declines, and equals only
    /// an instance of its class whose slots are equal.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other` is of a class equal to the receiver's and
    /// its slots, in declaration order, are equal to the receiver's, each
    /// compared with `=`; a Boolean.
    /// @signals Error 'cannot compare an instance of …: it is still under
    /// construction, and an instance of a value class is a value only once
    /// its factory has returned', naming the class, while the receiver is
    /// under construction, or `other` is and its class could be equal to
    /// the receiver's -- an `other` of a class that cannot is unequal
    /// whatever its state, and answered `false` -- and likewise for each
    /// pair of slots the comparison reaches before it finds an unequal
    /// one, the receiver's slot in the receiver's part, but for a pair
    /// whose receiver's slot is of a class that declares its own `=`:
    /// that `=` compares the pair, and decides for itself.
    /// @see `valueEquals:` and `Object>>=`.
    public = other = (
      ^vmMirror value: self sameAs: other ifFail: [^self valueEquals: other].
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by sending `=`: a value has no
    /// identity of its own.
    ///
    /// §3.1.1: where `Value` declares an identity method, "that method
    /// must return the same results as `Value`'s equality method". A send
    /// of `=` makes that true by construction, for every subclass.
    ///
    /// So two equal values are identical, whichever class decides their
    /// equality: `'abc' == ('abcd' copyFrom: 1 to: 3)`, a String a
    /// StringBuilder built with the literal of its text, two equal
    /// LargeIntegers, `1 == 1.0`, `0.0 == -0.0`, a NaN with every NaN, and
    /// two instances of a program's value class whose slots are equal --
    /// all true. Identity is then exactly as much an equivalence as `=`
    /// is. A subclass may not declare `==` of its own: §3.1.1 makes it no
    /// value class, and the runtime refuses to instantiate it.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers what `=` answers; a subclass's own `=` decides it, so it
    /// is a Boolean only where that `=` answers one.
    /// @signals Error what `=` signals. Where `=` is `Value`'s own, the
    /// derived equality, it signals for an instance under construction:
    /// always for the receiver, and for the receiver's side of each pair
    /// of slots the comparison reaches; for `other`, and the other side of
    /// such a pair, only where its class could be equal to the one it is
    /// compared with, an `other` of a class that cannot being unequal and
    /// answered `false`. An instance whose factory has not returned has no
    /// identity yet. A value class that declares an `=` of its own decides
    /// for itself, and its `==` signals only where that `=` does; so does
    /// a pair of slots whose receiver's slot is of such a class.
    /// @see `=` and `Object>>==`.
    public == other = (
      ^self = other.
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver by its slots, so that the hash agrees with
    /// `=`.
    ///
    /// It reads only the state `=` compares, never the object's identity,
    /// so a copy of a value hashes like its original, and two values `=`
    /// makes equal hash alike whatever their slots' classes: a number slot
    /// hashes by its exact value, so `3` and `3.0` count alike. The class
    /// does not enter it, so instances of two classes with equal slots may
    /// hash alike, which the contract allows. The leaf hashes every slot it
    /// can by the rule of the slot's own class; where a slot's class
    /// declares an `=` of its own it declines, as `=`'s leaf does on the
    /// same values, and the answer is the slot count plus each slot's own
    /// `hash` -- so a value whose slot's class declares `=` relies on that
    /// class's `hash` agreeing with it. A value never changes, and neither
    /// does its hash.
    ///
    /// @answers an Integer computed from the slots, alike for every two
    /// values `=` makes equal.
    /// @signals Error 'cannot hash an instance of …: it is still under
    /// construction, and an instance of a value class is a value only once
    /// its factory has returned', naming the class, while the receiver or a
    /// value its slots reach is under construction: a hash of the slots
    /// built so far would change once the rest were. A slot of a class
    /// that declares its own `=` is hashed by its own `hash`, which decides
    /// for itself.
    /// @see `=` and `Object>>hash`.
    public hash = (
      ^vmMirror valueHash: self ifFail: [^self valueHashOfSlots].
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver as `hash` does: a value's identity is its
    /// equality.
    ///
    /// Two equal values are identical by `==`, so their identity hashes
    /// must agree, and only a hash of the state does: an identity hash
    /// drawn per object would split equal values. An immediate -- a
    /// SmallInteger, a SmallFloat64, `true`, `false`, `nil` -- answers its
    /// `hash` too: `3 identityHash` is 3, `nil identityHash` 0. An
    /// `IdentityMap`, which finds a key by `identityHash` and compares it
    /// with `==`, therefore holds equal values as one key, as a `Map` does:
    /// `1` and `1.0` are one key of either, and so are a String and a copy
    /// of it.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver's `hash`.
    /// @signals Error what `hash` signals, while the receiver or a value
    /// its slots reach is under construction.
    /// @see `hash` and `Object>>identityHash`.
    public identityHash = (
      ^self hash.
    )

    /// The equality `=` leaves to Newspeak, comparing the slots in order.
    /// It reads `other`'s class through the mirror, since `class` is
    /// answered only to its receiver's own sends.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers whether `other`'s class is `==` the receiver's and each of
    /// its slots is `=` the receiver's slot at the same position, from the
    /// first; a Boolean.
    private valueEquals: other = (
      |
        index ::= 1.
        count
      |
      (self class == (vmMirror objectClass: other ifFail: [^false])) ifFalse: [^false].
      count:: self valueSlotCountOf: self.
      (count = (self valueSlotCountOf: other)) ifFalse: [^false].
      [index <= count] whileTrue: [
        ((self valueSlotOf: self at: index) = (self valueSlotOf: other at: index)) ifFalse: [
          ^false.
        ].
        index:: index + 1.
      ].
      ^true.
    )

    /// The hash `hash` leaves to Newspeak, sending `hash` to every slot,
    /// so that it agrees with `valueEquals:`.
    ///
    /// @answers the slot count plus the `hash` of every slot.
    private valueHashOfSlots = (
      |
        count = self valueSlotCountOf: self.
        result ::= count.
        index ::= 1.
      |
      [index <= count] whileTrue: [
        result:: result + (self valueSlotOf: self at: index) hash.
        index:: index + 1.
      ].
      ^result.
    )

    /// Counts the slots `subject`'s classes declare, through the VM
    /// mirror.
    ///
    /// Both name their subject rather than forwarding `self`: §5.9
    /// reaches a private member only through a lexical send, so
    /// `other valueSlotCount` would find nothing, and the other side's
    /// state has to be read through the same leaf as this side's. That
    /// costs each of them the primitive-method fast path, which only
    /// the declining path pays.
    ///
    /// @param subject the receiver or the object it is compared with.
    /// @answers the number of slots `subject`'s classes declare, 0 for a
    /// `subject` whose state is no slots.
    private valueSlotCountOf: subject = (
      ^vmMirror valueSlotCount: subject ifFail: [^0].
    )

    /// Reads `subject`'s declared slot at `index`, through the same leaf
    /// for either side.
    ///
    /// @param subject as `valueSlotCountOf:`'s.
    /// @param index an Integer from 1 to that slot count.
    /// @answers `subject`'s declared slot at `index`, counted from 1 in
    /// declaration order; nil for a `subject` whose state is no slots.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `index`, 'index … out of range:
    /// valid indices are 1..size (size …)', for an Integer outside that
    /// range.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `index`, 'slotAt: requires an
    /// Integer index, got …', for one that is no Integer.
    /// @see `valueSlotCountOf:`.
    private valueSlotOf: subject at: index = (
      ^vmMirror value: subject slotAt: index ifFail: [^nil].
    )
  )

  /// The superclass of `Integer`, `Float` and `Fraction`: counting
  /// loops, comparing, the mathematical functions through `asFloat`, and
  /// the questions about NaN and infinity.
  ///
  /// §3.1.1 names `Number` among the classes whose instances are values,
  /// and §5.1.1's numeric literals are its instances. Iteration is here
  /// so that an `Integer`, a `LargeInteger` and a `Float` receiver share
  /// one body. The compiler does not inline them; they are sent.
  ///
  /// A receiver holding no number -- `Number new`, `Fraction new`, an
  /// instance of a program's subclass of `Number` -- makes every leaf
  /// decline: a send that no other method above the leaf's answers ends
  /// in `doesNotUnderstand:`, a MessageNotUnderstood, as does a method
  /// written in Newspeak whose own sends reach such a leaf. The Errors
  /// each method below names are those of a receiver holding a number.
  public class Number = Value () (
    /// Identifies the receiver as a Number, without a class test.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isKindOfNumber = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Identifies the receiver as no Float: a Number is no Float unless
    /// its class says so.
    ///
    /// @answers false.
    public isKindOfFloat = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Identifies the receiver as no Integer: a Number is no Integer
    /// unless its class says so.
    ///
    /// @answers false.
    public isKindOfInteger = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Identifies the receiver as no Fraction: a Number is no Fraction
    /// unless its class says so.
    ///
    /// @answers false.
    public isKindOfFraction = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by their exact values.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is less than `other`, a Boolean:
    /// false whenever either is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '< requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public < other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self lessThan: other ifFail: [^super < other].
    )

    /// As `<`, whether the receiver is greater.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is greater than `other`, a Boolean:
    /// false whenever either is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '> requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `<`.
    public > other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self greaterThan: other ifFail: [^super > other].
    )

    /// As `<`, whether the receiver is less or equal.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is less than or equal to `other`, a
    /// Boolean: false whenever either is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '<= requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `<`.
    public <= other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self lessOrEqual: other ifFail: [^super <= other].
    )

    /// As `<`, whether the receiver is greater or equal.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is greater than or equal to `other`,
    /// a Boolean: false whenever either is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '>= requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `<`.
    public >= other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self greaterOrEqual: other ifFail: [^super >= other].
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by exact value, whatever its
    /// class: `1 = 1.0`, `(1/2) = 0.5`; a NaN equals a NaN. It never
    /// signals.
    ///
    /// The numbers are the one family of classes whose `=` is true across
    /// classes: an Integer, a LargeInteger, a Float and a Fraction are
    /// equal when they denote the same number, compared exactly -- a Float
    /// is the binary fraction its bits denote, so `0.1 = (1/10)` is false
    /// and an integer beyond 2^53 equals only the Float of exactly its
    /// value. A LargeInteger is never equal to a SmallInteger, whose
    /// range of values it lies outside. Between two Floats it is IEEE
    /// 754's equality but for one case: a NaN equals every NaN and nothing
    /// else, so that `=` stays reflexive. `0.0` and `-0.0` are equal, as
    /// IEEE has them, and equal to `0`; an infinity equals only the
    /// infinity of its sign. Across classes it differs from IEEE's
    /// comparison after a conversion to Float, since it converts nothing:
    /// `(10 raisedTo: 400) = (1.0 / 0.0)` is false, and so is
    /// `9007199254740993 = 9007199254740993 asFloat`, 2^53 + 1 against the
    /// Float it rounds to. `<` and its siblings compare exactly across
    /// classes too, and agree with IEEE only in answering false for a NaN.
    /// So the equality is an equivalence -- reflexive, symmetric,
    /// transitive -- whichever side is which class, and `hash` agrees with
    /// it. Through `Value>>==`, `==` answers the same: `1 == 1.0` and a
    /// NaN with itself are true.
    ///
    /// A receiver that holds no number -- `Number new`, `Fraction new`,
    /// an instance of a program's subclass -- is compared by
    /// `Value>>=` instead, by class and slots; it equals no number.
    ///
    /// @param other any object; anything that is no number is unequal.
    /// @answers whether `other` is a number of the same exact value, a
    /// Boolean.
    /// @see `hash`, `Value>>=` and `Object>>=`.
    public = other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self equals: other ifFail: [^super = other].
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver by its exact value, so that the hash agrees
    /// with `=` whatever the classes; an integer's is the integer
    /// itself.
    ///
    /// Every number's hash is its exact value reduced modulo the prime
    /// 2^61 - 1, signed as the value is and folded into the SmallInteger
    /// range: a SmallInteger hashes as itself, and `3`, `3.0` and `(6/2)`,
    /// `0.5` and `(1/2)` hash alike, as `=` makes them equal. `0.0` and
    /// `-0.0` hash as `0`; every NaN answers one constant and each
    /// infinity another, its sign's, which a Fraction whose denominator is
    /// a multiple of 2^61 - 1 answers too, having no inverse to reduce by.
    /// Unequal numbers may share a hash -- a LargeInteger and the
    /// SmallInteger it is congruent to, for one -- which the contract
    /// allows. A number never changes, and neither does
    /// its hash; `identityHash` answers it too (`Value>>identityHash`).
    ///
    /// @answers an Integer, alike for every two numbers `=` makes equal.
    /// @see `=` and `Object>>hash`.
    public hash = (
      ^vmMirror numberHash: self ifFail: [^super hash].
    )

    /// Adds `other` to the receiver: exactly when neither is a Float, by
    /// IEEE 754 when either is.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the sum of the receiver and `other`: exact when neither
    /// is a Float -- an integer when it is integral, a Fraction otherwise
    /// -- and a Float computed by IEEE 754 when either is, the other
    /// rounded to the nearest Float first.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '+ requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public + other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self add: other ifFail: [^super + other].
    )

    /// As `+`, the difference.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the difference of the receiver and `other`, exact or a
    /// Float as `+`'s sum is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '- requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `+`.
    public - other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self subtract: other ifFail: [^super - other].
    )

    /// As `+`, the product.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the product of the receiver and `other`, exact or a Float
    /// as `+`'s sum is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '* requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `+`.
    public * other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self multiply: other ifFail: [^super * other].
    )

    /// As `+`, the quotient: exact when neither is a Float, so `7 / 2`
    /// is the Fraction (7/2) and `6 / 3` the Integer 2; a Float when
    /// either is, an infinity or a NaN for a zero divisor.
    ///
    /// @param other a number; not an exact zero when neither is a Float.
    /// @answers the quotient of the receiver by `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// an exact zero `other` when neither is a Float.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '/ requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `+`.
    public / other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self divide: other ifFail: [^super / other].
    )

    /// Divides the receiver by `other`, rounding the quotient toward
    /// negative infinity.
    ///
    /// @param other a number other than zero, 0.0 included.
    /// @answers the quotient of the receiver by `other` rounded toward
    /// negative infinity: an integer when neither is a Float, a Float
    /// when either is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// a zero `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '// requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public // other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self floorDivide: other ifFail: [^super // other].
    )

    /// Takes the remainder of `//`, `self - (self // other * other)`,
    /// which has the sign of `other`.
    ///
    /// @param other as `//`'s.
    /// @answers the remainder of `//`: exact when neither is a Float, a
    /// Float when either is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// a zero `other`, as `//` does.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '\\ requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `//`.
    public \\ other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self modulo: other ifFail: [^super \\ other].
    )

    /// Divides the receiver by `other`, rounding the quotient toward
    /// zero.
    ///
    /// @param other as `//`'s.
    /// @answers the quotient of the receiver by `other` rounded toward
    /// zero: an integer when neither is a Float, a Float when either is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// a zero `other`, as `//` does.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'quo: requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `//`.
    public quo: other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self quotient: other ifFail: [^super quo: other].
    )

    /// Takes the remainder of `quo:`,
    /// `self - ((self quo: other) * other)`, which has the sign of the
    /// receiver.
    ///
    /// @param other as `//`'s.
    /// @answers the remainder of `quo:`: exact when neither is a Float, a
    /// Float when either is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// a zero `other`, as `//` does.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'rem: requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `//`.
    public rem: other = (
      ^vmMirror number: self remainder: other ifFail: [^super rem: other].
    )

    /// Converts the receiver to the nearest Float, a tie rounded to the
    /// even one.
    ///
    /// @answers the nearest Float to the receiver's exact value: an
    /// infinity beyond the largest Float, a zero below half the
    /// smallest.
    public asFloat = (
      ^vmMirror numberAsFloat: self ifFail: [^super asFloat].
    )

    /// Negates the receiver exactly.
    ///
    /// @answers `0 - self`.
    public negated = (
      ^0 - self.
    )

    /// Drops the receiver's sign.
    ///
    /// @answers `negated` when the receiver is below 0, the receiver
    /// otherwise.
    public abs = (
      ^self < 0 ifTrue: [self negated] ifFalse: [self].
    )

    /// Raises the receiver to `exponent`: exactly by repeated squaring
    /// for an integer `exponent` -- a negative one answering the
    /// reciprocal, a Fraction for an integer receiver other than 1 and
    /// -1 -- and through `asFloat` and `Float>>raisedTo:` for any other.
    ///
    /// @param exponent a number.
    /// @answers the receiver raised to `exponent`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError of `/`, 'division by zero', for a zero
    /// receiver and a negative integer `exponent`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError that `Float>>raisedTo:` signals for an
    /// `exponent` that is no number.
    /// @see `Float>>raisedTo:`.
    public raisedTo: exponent = (
      |
        result ::= 1.
        base ::= self.
        remaining ::= exponent.
      |
      exponent isKindOfInteger ifFalse: [^self asFloat raisedTo: exponent].
      exponent < 0 ifTrue: [^1 / (self raisedTo: exponent negated)].
      [remaining > 0] whileTrue: [
        (remaining bitAnd: 1) = 1 ifTrue: [result:: result * base].
        remaining:: remaining >> 1.
        remaining > 0 ifTrue: [base:: base * base].
      ].
      ^result.
    )

    /// Evaluates `action` with the receiver, then with each number one
    /// greater, in increasing order, while it is at most `stop`. Nothing
    /// is evaluated for a receiver above `stop`.
    ///
    /// @param stop a number; compared with `<=`.
    /// @param action a block of one argument, or anything understanding
    /// `value:`; sent `value:` with each number.
    /// @answers nil.
    /// @signals ArgumentError the receiver's `<=` signals for a `stop`
    /// that is no number.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 1', for a block that does not take
    /// one argument, when the receiver is at most `stop`.
    public to: stop do: action = (
      |
        current ::= self.
      |
      [current <= stop] whileTrue: [
        action value: current.
        current:: current + 1.
      ].
      ^nil.
    )

    /// Evaluates `action` with the receiver, then with each number
    /// `step` further on, while it is at most `stop` -- at least `stop`
    /// for a negative `step`.
    ///
    /// @param stop a number; compared with `<=`, or `>=` for a negative
    /// `step`.
    /// @param step a number other than zero, sent `<` with 0 first; a
    /// zero `step` never ends from a receiver at most `stop`.
    /// @param action as `to:do:`'s.
    /// @answers nil.
    /// @signals ArgumentError the receiver's comparison signals for a
    /// `stop` that is no number.
    /// @signals ArgumentError of `to:do:` for a block that does not take
    /// one argument, when the loop runs a step.
    /// @see `to:do:`.
    public to: stop by: step do: action = (
      |
        current ::= self.
      |
      step < 0 ifTrue: [
        [current >= stop] whileTrue: [
          action value: current.
          current:: current + step.
        ].
      ] ifFalse: [
        [current <= stop] whileTrue: [
          action value: current.
          current:: current + step.
        ].
      ].
      ^nil.
    )

    /// Picks the greater of the receiver and `other`.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the receiver when it is `>` `other`, `other` otherwise.
    /// @signals ArgumentError the receiver's `>` signals for an `other`
    /// that is no number.
    public max: other = (
      ^self > other ifTrue: [self] ifFalse: [other].
    )

    /// Picks the lesser of the receiver and `other`.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the receiver when it is `<` `other`, `other` otherwise.
    /// @signals ArgumentError the receiver's `<` signals for an `other`
    /// that is no number.
    public min: other = (
      ^self < other ifTrue: [self] ifFalse: [other].
    )

    /// Tests whether the receiver lies between `min` and `max`, both
    /// included.
    ///
    /// @param min a number.
    /// @param max a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is at least `min` and at most `max`:
    /// a Boolean for every number, whose `>=` and `<=` answer one; a
    /// subclass's own `>=` and `<=` decide it otherwise.
    /// @signals ArgumentError the receiver's `>=` or `<=` signals for a
    /// bound that is no number.
    public between: min and: max = (
      ^self >= min and: [self <= max].
    )

    /// Tells whether the receiver is a NaN: only a Float can be one.
    ///
    /// @answers false.
    public isNaN = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Tells whether the receiver is finite: only a Float can be
    /// infinite or a NaN.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isFinite = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Tells whether the receiver is infinite: only a Float can be.
    ///
    /// @answers false.
    public isInfinite = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Takes the square root of the receiver, computed on `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the square root of the receiver as a Float.
    public sqrt = (
      ^self asFloat sqrt.
    )

    /// Takes the sine of the receiver in radians, computed on `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the sine of the receiver as a Float.
    public sin = (
      ^self asFloat sin.
    )

    /// Takes the cosine of the receiver in radians, computed on
    /// `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the cosine of the receiver as a Float.
    public cos = (
      ^self asFloat cos.
    )

    /// Takes the tangent of the receiver in radians, computed on
    /// `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the tangent of the receiver as a Float.
    public tan = (
      ^self asFloat tan.
    )

    /// Takes the arc sine of the receiver, computed on `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the arc sine of the receiver as a Float.
    public asin = (
      ^self asFloat asin.
    )

    /// Takes the arc cosine of the receiver, computed on `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the arc cosine of the receiver as a Float.
    public acos = (
      ^self asFloat acos.
    )

    /// Takes the arc tangent of the receiver, computed on `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the arc tangent of the receiver as a Float.
    public atan = (
      ^self asFloat atan.
    )

    /// Sends `Float>>atan:` to the receiver's `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @param x a number.
    /// @answers what `Float>>atan:` answers for the receiver's `asFloat`
    /// and `x`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError of `Float>>atan:` for any other argument.
    /// @see `Float>>atan:`.
    public atan: x = (
      ^self asFloat atan: x.
    )

    /// Raises e to the receiver, computed on `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers e raised to the receiver as a Float.
    public exp = (
      ^self asFloat exp.
    )

    /// Takes the natural logarithm of the receiver, computed on
    /// `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the natural logarithm of the receiver as a Float.
    public ln = (
      ^self asFloat ln.
    )

    /// Takes the logarithm to base 10 of the receiver, computed on
    /// `asFloat`.
    ///
    /// @answers the logarithm to base 10 of the receiver as a Float.
    public log = (
      ^self asFloat log.
    )
  )

  /// The superclass of `True` and `False`.
  ///
  /// It declares only `isKindOfBoolean`: every conditional and logical
  /// message is answered by a method of `True` or of `False`.
  public class Boolean = Value () (
    /// Identifies the receiver as a Boolean, without a class test.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isKindOfBoolean = (
      ^true.
    )
  )

  /// The class of `true`.
  ///
  /// §5.1.2: `true` is the unique instance of `True` and `false` of
  /// `False`. Each branch is a method of its own, so no method tests the
  /// receiver and none reaches the mirror. Every body is `^true`,
  /// `^false`, `^nil`, `^<parameter>` or `^<parameter> value`: a quick
  /// method, which runs without an activation, so that the
  /// reference lowering, which sends a conditional the optimized lowering
  /// inlines, counts no activation for it either
  /// (`docs/design/core-library.md`, "`True` and `False` are quick
  /// methods"). An argument that is no block is sent `value` like any
  /// other object; `printString`, `asString` and `hash` alone are
  /// ordinary methods.
  public class True = Boolean () (
    /// Evaluates `trueBlock`; `falseBlock` is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param trueBlock a block of no argument, or anything understanding
    /// `value`; sent `value`.
    /// @param falseBlock any object.
    /// @answers the value of `trueBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a `trueBlock` that declares
    /// any parameter.
    public ifTrue: trueBlock ifFalse: falseBlock = (
      ^trueBlock value.
    )

    /// As `ifTrue:ifFalse:`, its arguments in the other order.
    ///
    /// @param falseBlock any object.
    /// @param trueBlock as `ifTrue:ifFalse:`'s.
    /// @answers the value of `trueBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a `trueBlock` that declares
    /// any parameter.
    /// @see `ifTrue:ifFalse:`.
    public ifFalse: falseBlock ifTrue: trueBlock = (
      ^trueBlock value.
    )

    /// Evaluates `trueBlock`, sending it `value`.
    ///
    /// @param trueBlock as `ifTrue:ifFalse:`'s.
    /// @answers the value of `trueBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a `trueBlock` that declares
    /// any parameter.
    /// @see `ifTrue:ifFalse:`.
    public ifTrue: trueBlock = (
      ^trueBlock value.
    )

    /// Skips `falseBlock`, which is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param falseBlock any object.
    /// @answers nil.
    public ifFalse: falseBlock = (
      ^nil.
    )

    /// Negates the receiver.
    ///
    /// @answers false.
    public not = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Ands the receiver with `other`, which is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers `other`, unevaluated.
    public & other = (
      ^other.
    )

    /// Ands the receiver with the value of `block`, sent `value`.
    ///
    /// @param block a block of no argument, or anything understanding
    /// `value`.
    /// @answers the value of `block`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a block that declares any
    /// parameter.
    public and: block = (
      ^block value.
    )

    /// Ors the receiver with `other`, which is not looked at.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers true.
    public | other = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Ors the receiver with `block`, which is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param block any object.
    /// @answers true.
    public or: block = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Prints the receiver as its literal.
    ///
    /// @answers the String 'true'.
    public printString = (
      ^'true'.
    )

    /// Names the receiver as its literal, as `printString` does.
    ///
    /// @answers the String 'true'.
    /// @see `printString`.
    public asString = (
      ^'true'.
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver to a constant, unlike `false`'s.
    ///
    /// `true` is equal only to itself (`Value>>=`), so any constant agrees
    /// with `=`; its `identityHash` is this hash too.
    ///
    /// @answers 1.
    /// @see `Object>>hash`.
    public hash = (
      ^1.
    )
  )

  /// The class of `false`.
  ///
  /// Its methods are `True`'s with the branches exchanged, each a quick
  /// method but `printString`, `asString` and `hash`, as `True`'s are.
  public class False = Boolean () (
    /// Evaluates `falseBlock`; `trueBlock` is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param trueBlock any object.
    /// @param falseBlock a block of no argument, or anything
    /// understanding `value`; sent `value`.
    /// @answers the value of `falseBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a `falseBlock` that declares
    /// any parameter.
    public ifTrue: trueBlock ifFalse: falseBlock = (
      ^falseBlock value.
    )

    /// As `ifTrue:ifFalse:`, its arguments in the other order.
    ///
    /// @param falseBlock as `ifTrue:ifFalse:`'s.
    /// @param trueBlock any object.
    /// @answers the value of `falseBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a `falseBlock` that declares
    /// any parameter.
    /// @see `ifTrue:ifFalse:`.
    public ifFalse: falseBlock ifTrue: trueBlock = (
      ^falseBlock value.
    )

    /// Skips `trueBlock`, which is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param trueBlock any object.
    /// @answers nil.
    public ifTrue: trueBlock = (
      ^nil.
    )

    /// Evaluates `falseBlock`, sending it `value`.
    ///
    /// @param falseBlock as `ifTrue:ifFalse:`'s.
    /// @answers the value of `falseBlock`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a `falseBlock` that declares
    /// any parameter.
    /// @see `ifTrue:ifFalse:`.
    public ifFalse: falseBlock = (
      ^falseBlock value.
    )

    /// Negates the receiver.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public not = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Ands the receiver with `other`, which is not looked at.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers false.
    public & other = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Ands the receiver with `block`, which is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param block any object.
    /// @answers false.
    public and: block = (
      ^false.
    )

    /// Ors the receiver with `other`, which is not evaluated.
    ///
    /// @param other any object.
    /// @answers `other`, unevaluated.
    public | other = (
      ^other.
    )

    /// Ors the receiver with the value of `block`, sent `value`.
    ///
    /// @param block a block of no argument, or anything understanding
    /// `value`.
    /// @answers the value of `block`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a block that declares any
    /// parameter.
    public or: block = (
      ^block value.
    )

    /// Prints the receiver as its literal.
    ///
    /// @answers the String 'false'.
    public printString = (
      ^'false'.
    )

    /// Names the receiver as its literal, as `printString` does.
    ///
    /// @answers the String 'false'.
    /// @see `printString`.
    public asString = (
      ^'false'.
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver to a constant, unlike `true`'s.
    ///
    /// `false` is equal only to itself (`Value>>=`), so any constant
    /// agrees with `=`; its `identityHash` is this hash too.
    ///
    /// @answers 0.
    /// @see `Object>>hash`.
    public hash = (
      ^0.
    )
  )

  /// The integers, of any size: a literal such as `3` is one, and an
  /// integer outside -2^60 to 2^60 - 1 is a `LargeInteger`.
  public class Integer = Number () (
    /// Identifies the receiver as an Integer, without a class test.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isKindOfInteger = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Adds `other` to the receiver, exactly unless `other` is a Float.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the sum of the receiver and `other`: an integer for an
    /// integer `other`, a LargeInteger only when it lies outside -2^60
    /// to 2^60 - 1; exactly, a Fraction or an integer, for a Fraction
    /// `other`; and a Float for a Float `other`, the receiver converted
    /// to the nearest Float first.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '+ requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public + other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self add: other ifFail: [^super + other].
    )

    /// As `+`, the difference.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the difference of the receiver and `other`: an integer,
    /// a Fraction or a Float as `+`'s sum is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '- requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `+`.
    public - other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self subtract: other ifFail: [^super - other].
    )

    /// As `+`, the product.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers the product of the receiver and `other`: an integer, a
    /// Fraction or a Float as `+`'s sum is.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '* requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `+`.
    public * other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self multiply: other ifFail: [^super * other].
    )

    /// Divides the receiver by `other`, rounding the quotient toward
    /// negative infinity.
    ///
    /// @param other a number other than zero, 0.0 included.
    /// @answers the quotient of the receiver by `other` rounded toward
    /// negative infinity: an integer for an integer or a Fraction
    /// `other`, a Float for a Float `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// a zero `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '// requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public // other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self floorDivide: other ifFail: [^super // other].
    )

    /// Takes the remainder of `//`, `self - (self // other * other)`,
    /// which has the sign of `other`.
    ///
    /// @param other as `//`'s.
    /// @answers the remainder of `//`: exact for an integer or a
    /// Fraction `other`, a Float for a Float `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError 'division by zero', carrying `other`, for
    /// a zero `other`, as `//` does.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '\\ requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `//`.
    public \\ other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self modulo: other ifFail: [^super \\ other].
    )

    /// Ands the receiver and `other` bitwise, on the two's-complement
    /// form of integers of any size.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger.
    /// @answers the bitwise and of the receiver and `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'bitAnd: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public bitAnd: other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self bitAnd: other ifFail: [^super bitAnd: other].
    )

    /// As `bitAnd:`, the bitwise or.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger.
    /// @answers the bitwise or of the receiver and `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'bitOr: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `bitAnd:`.
    public bitOr: other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self bitOr: other ifFail: [^super bitOr: other].
    )

    /// As `bitAnd:`, the bitwise exclusive or.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger.
    /// @answers the bitwise exclusive or of the receiver and `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'bitXor: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `bitAnd:`.
    public bitXor: other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self bitXor: other ifFail: [^super bitXor: other].
    )

    /// Shifts the receiver left by `other` bits.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger, at least 0.
    /// @answers the receiver times 2 to the power `other`; 0 for a zero
    /// receiver, whatever non-negative count.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '<< requires an integer
    /// argument, got …', for an argument that is no integer.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '<< takes a non-negative
    /// shift count, got …', for a negative one.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '<< cannot allocate the
    /// result of shifting by … bits', for a result too large to
    /// allocate.
    public << other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self shiftLeft: other ifFail: [^super << other].
    )

    /// Shifts the receiver right by `count` bits: the two's-complement
    /// shift, so a negative receiver shifts to -1 at the least.
    ///
    /// @param count an Integer or a LargeInteger, at least 0.
    /// @answers the receiver divided by 2 to the power `count`, rounded
    /// toward negative infinity.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `count`, '>> requires an integer
    /// argument, got …', for an argument that is no integer.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `count`, '>> takes a non-negative
    /// shift count, got …', for a negative one.
    public >> count = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self shiftRight: count ifFail: [^super >> count].
    )

    /// Ands the receiver and `other` bitwise over their two's
    /// complements, as `bitAnd:` does.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger.
    /// @answers the bitwise and of the receiver and `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'bitAnd: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for any other argument; the text names
    /// 'bitAnd:', not '&'.
    /// @see `bitAnd:`.
    public & other = (
      ^self bitAnd: other.
    )

    /// Ors the receiver and `other` bitwise, inclusively, over their
    /// two's complements, as `bitOr:` does.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger.
    /// @answers the bitwise inclusive or of the receiver and `other`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'bitOr: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for any other argument; the text names
    /// 'bitOr:', not '|'.
    /// @see `bitOr:`.
    public | other = (
      ^self bitOr: other.
    )

    /// Finds the greatest common divisor of the receiver and `other`.
    ///
    /// @param other an Integer or a LargeInteger.
    /// @answers the greatest common divisor, an integer never negative;
    /// 0 when both are 0.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, 'gcd: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public gcd: other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self gcd: other ifFail: [^super gcd: other].
    )

    /// Reads a base-256 digit of the receiver's magnitude, least
    /// significant first: the sign is not a digit, so `-1 digitAt: 1`
    /// is 1.
    ///
    /// @param index an Integer from 1 to `digitLength`.
    /// @answers the digit at `index`, an Integer from 0 to 255.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `index`, 'digitAt: index … out of
    /// range: valid indices are 1..… (digitLength …)', for an Integer
    /// outside them, a LargeInteger included.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `index`, 'digitAt: requires an
    /// integer argument, got …', for one that is no integer.
    public digitAt: index = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self digitAt: index ifFail: [^super digitAt: index].
    )

    /// Counts the base-256 digits of the receiver's magnitude.
    ///
    /// @answers how many digits the magnitude has, an Integer: 0 for 0.
    public digitLength = (
      ^vmMirror integerDigitLength: self ifFail: [^super digitLength].
    )

    /// Prints the receiver in `radix`: its digits from '0' to '9' and
    /// 'A' to 'Z', most significant first, after a '-' for a negative
    /// receiver.
    ///
    /// @param radix an Integer from 2 to 36.
    /// @answers a new String of the receiver in `radix`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `radix`, 'asStringRadix: takes a
    /// radix from 2 to 36, got …', for an Integer outside them, a
    /// LargeInteger included.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `radix`, 'asStringRadix: requires
    /// an integer argument, got …', for one that is no integer.
    public asStringRadix: radix = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self asStringRadix: radix ifFail: [^super asStringRadix: radix].
    )

    /// Rounds the receiver toward negative infinity: an integer is its
    /// own floor.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public floor = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Rounds the receiver toward positive infinity: an integer is its
    /// own ceiling.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public ceiling = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Rounds the receiver: an integer is already rounded.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public rounded = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Truncates the receiver: an integer has no fraction.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public truncated = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Converts the receiver to an integer, which it already is.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public asInteger = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Reads the receiver's numerator: an integer is the numerator of
    /// the fraction it is, over 1.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    public numerator = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Reads the receiver's denominator: an integer is a fraction whose
    /// denominator is 1.
    ///
    /// @answers 1.
    public denominator = (
      ^1.
    )

    /// Identifies the receiver as a fraction: an integer is a fraction
    /// whose denominator is 1.
    ///
    /// @answers true.
    public isKindOfFraction = (
      ^true.
    )

    /// Raises the receiver to `exponent` modulo `modulus`, by squaring
    /// and multiplying modulo `modulus` at each step; a negative
    /// `exponent` raises the receiver's inverse modulo `modulus` to
    /// `exponent negated`.
    ///
    /// An `exponent` that is no integer is not refused by an Error of
    /// its own: the first send to fail is `<` (for `nil`) or `bitAnd:`
    /// (for `2.5`), each a MessageNotUnderstood.
    ///
    /// @param exponent an integer.
    /// @param modulus an integer other than 0.
    /// @answers the receiver raised to `exponent` modulo `modulus`, an
    /// integer from 0 to `modulus` - 1 for a positive `modulus`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError of `\\` for a `modulus` that is no number
    /// or is 0.
    /// @signals ArgumentError that `reciprocalModulo:` signals for a
    /// negative `exponent` when the receiver has no inverse.
    /// @see `reciprocalModulo:`.
    public raisedTo: exponent modulo: modulus = (
      |
        result
        base
        remaining ::= exponent.
      |
      exponent < 0 ifTrue: [
        ^(self reciprocalModulo: modulus) raisedTo: exponent negated modulo: modulus.
      ].
      result:: 1 \\ modulus.
      base:: self \\ modulus.
      [remaining > 0] whileTrue: [
        (remaining bitAnd: 1) = 1 ifTrue: [result:: result * base \\ modulus].
        base:: base * base \\ modulus.
        remaining:: remaining >> 1.
      ].
      ^result.
    )

    /// Finds the inverse of the receiver modulo `modulus` by the
    /// extended Euclidean algorithm.
    ///
    /// @param modulus a positive integer.
    /// @answers the integer from 0 to `modulus` - 1 whose product with
    /// the receiver is 1 modulo `modulus`.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `modulus`, 'reciprocalModulo: …
    /// has no inverse modulo …', when the receiver and `modulus` have a
    /// common divisor other than 1.
    /// @signals ArgumentError of `\\` for a `modulus` that is no number
    /// or is 0 (the first send to fail, so `//` never sees it).
    public reciprocalModulo: modulus = (
      |
        remainder ::= modulus.
        nextRemainder ::= self \\ modulus.
        coefficient ::= 0.
        nextCoefficient ::= 1.
      |
      [nextRemainder = 0] whileFalse: [
        |
          quotient = remainder // nextRemainder.
          previousRemainder = remainder.
          previousCoefficient
        |
        remainder:: nextRemainder.
        nextRemainder:: previousRemainder - (quotient * nextRemainder).
        previousCoefficient:: coefficient.
        coefficient:: nextCoefficient.
        nextCoefficient:: previousCoefficient - (quotient * nextCoefficient).
      ].
      remainder = 1 ifFalse: [
        ^(outer Kernel ArgumentError value: modulus) signal: 'reciprocalModulo: '
            , self printString
            , ' has no inverse modulo '
            , modulus printString.
      ].
      ^coefficient \\ modulus.
    )

    /// Complements the receiver bitwise.
    ///
    /// @answers `-1 - self`.
    public bitInvert = (
      ^-1 - self.
    )

    /// Evaluates `action` as many times as the receiver says, none for
    /// a receiver below 1.
    ///
    /// The compiler does not inline it; it is sent.
    ///
    /// @param action a block of no argument, or anything understanding
    /// `value`; sent `value` each time.
    /// @answers nil.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying the block, 'wrong number of block
    /// arguments: block takes …, got 0', for a block that declares any
    /// parameter, when the receiver is at least 1.
    public timesRepeat: action = (
      1 to: self do: [:ignored | action value].
      ^nil.
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by their exact values, a Float
    /// or a Fraction `other` included, so 2^53 + 1 is greater than the
    /// Float 2^53.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is less than `other`, a Boolean:
    /// false whenever `other` is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '< requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    public < other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self lessThan: other ifFail: [^super < other].
    )

    /// As `<`, whether the receiver is greater.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is greater than `other`, a Boolean:
    /// false whenever `other` is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '> requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `<`.
    public > other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self greaterThan: other ifFail: [^super > other].
    )

    /// As `<`, whether the receiver is less or equal.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is less than or equal to `other`, a
    /// Boolean: false whenever `other` is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '<= requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `<`.
    public <= other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self lessOrEqual: other ifFail: [^super <= other].
    )

    /// As `<`, whether the receiver is greater or equal.
    ///
    /// @param other a number.
    /// @answers whether the receiver is greater than or equal to `other`,
    /// a Boolean: false whenever `other` is a NaN.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `other`, '>= requires a numeric
    /// argument, got …', for any other argument.
    /// @see `<`.
    public >= other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self greaterOrEqual: other ifFail: [^super >= other].
    )

    /// Compares the receiver with `other` by exact value: `3 = 3.0` is
    /// true. It never signals.
    ///
    /// `Number>>=`'s equality, answered here for a SmallInteger argument
    /// at once and for any other by the same rule, so that it stays
    /// symmetric with a Float's, a LargeInteger's and a Fraction's.
    ///
    /// @param other any object; anything that is no number is unequal.
    /// @answers whether `other` is a number of the same exact value, a
    /// Boolean.
    /// @see `Number>>=`.
    public = other = (
      ^vmMirror integer: self equals: other ifFail: [^super = other].
    )

    /// Hashes the receiver to itself, the hash every number of its value
    /// answers.
    ///
    /// `Number>>hash`'s rule leaves an integer in the SmallInteger range
    /// as it is, so `3.0 hash` is 3 too, and so is `3 identityHash`.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver.
    /// @see `Number>>hash`.
    public hash = (
      ^self.
    )

    /// Negates the receiver.
    ///
    /// @answers `0 - self`, an integer.
    public negated = (
      ^vmMirror integerNegated: self ifFail: [^super negated].
    )

    /// Drops the receiver's sign.
    ///
    /// @answers the receiver without its sign, an integer.
    public abs = (
      ^vmMirror integerAbsolute: self ifFail: [^super abs].
    )

    /// Converts the receiver to a Float, a tie rounded to the even one.
    ///
    /// @answers the nearest Float.
    public asFloat = (
      ^vmMirror integerAsFloat: self ifFail: [^super asFloat].
    )

    /// Prints the receiver in decimal.
    ///
    /// @answers a new String of the decimal digits, after a '-' for a
    /// negative receiver.
    public printString = (
      ^vmMirror integerPrintString: self ifFail: [^super printString].
    )

    /// Prints the receiver as `printString` does.
    ///
    /// @answers what `printString` answers.
    public asString = (
      ^vmMirror integerAsString: self ifFail: [^super asString].
    )
  ) : (
    /// Reads the Integer `text` writes in decimal, as `parse:radix:`
    /// does with radix 10.
    ///
    /// @param text a String.
    /// @answers what `parse:radix:` answers for `text` and radix 10.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `text`, 'parse:radix: requires a
    /// String, got …', for a `text` that is no String.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `text`, 'parse:radix: requires
    /// the digits of an Integer in radix 10, got …', for a String that
    /// writes no integer.
    /// @see `parse:radix:`.
    public parse: text = (
      ^self parse: text radix: 10.
    )

    /// Reads the Integer `text` writes in `radix`: an optional '-' and
    /// at least one digit, '0' to '9' then 'A' to 'Z' in either case,
    /// each below `radix`.
    ///
    /// The leaf answers only for `Integer` itself; any other receiver
    /// falls to `super`.
    ///
    /// @param text a String.
    /// @param radix an Integer from 2 to 36.
    /// @answers the integer `text` writes; a LargeInteger beyond -2^60
    /// to 2^60 - 1.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `radix`, 'parse:radix: takes a
    /// radix from 2 to 36, got …', for one outside them.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `text`, 'parse:radix: requires a
    /// String, got …', for one that is no String.
    /// @signals ArgumentError carrying `text`, 'parse:radix: requires
    /// the digits of an Integer in radix …, got …', for a String that
    /// writes no integer, an empty one or a lone '-' included.
    public parse: text radix: radix = (
      ^vmMirror integerClass: self parse: text radix: radix ifFail: [
        ^super parse: text radix: radix.
      ].
    )
  )

  /// An integer outside -2^60 to 2^60 - 1. It answers `Integer`'s
  /// protocol with the same answers and errors; any result within that
  /// range again is an `Integer`. It inherits `Integer`'s `gcd:`, `>>`,
  /// `digitAt:`, `digitLength`, `asStringRadix:` and the methods written
  /// in Newspeak, which take either representation.
  ///
  /// §5.1.1: "integers are not limited in size". An `Integer` whose value
  /// does not fit a `SmallInteger`; every leaf answers an `Integer` when
  /// the result fits again, so each integer has one representation.
